window.__FINDER__={"verses":{"matthew-6-34":{"ref":"Matthew 6:34","text":"Therefore don’t be anxious for tomorrow, for tomorrow will be anxious for itself. Each day’s own evil is sufficient."},"matthew-6-33":{"ref":"Matthew 6:33","text":"But seek first God’s Kingdom, and his righteousness; and all these things will be given to you as well."},"matthew-6-26":{"ref":"Matthew 6:26","text":"See the birds of the sky, that they don’t sow, neither do they reap, nor gather into barns. Your heavenly Father feeds them. Aren’t you of much more value than they?"},"matthew-11-28-29":{"ref":"Matthew 11:28-29","text":"Come to me, all you who labor and are heavily burdened, and I will give you rest. Take my yoke upon you, and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart; and you will find rest for your souls."},"matthew-5-4":{"ref":"Matthew 5:4","text":"Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted."},"matthew-6-14-15":{"ref":"Matthew 6:14-15","text":"For if you forgive men their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you. But if you don’t forgive men their trespasses, neither will your Father forgive your trespasses."},"matthew-18-21-22":{"ref":"Matthew 18:21-22","text":"Then Peter came and said to him, “Lord, how often shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? Until seven times?” Jesus said to him, “I don’t tell you until seven times, but, until seventy times seven."},"matthew-25-21":{"ref":"Matthew 25:21","text":"His lord said to him, ‘Well done, good and faithful servant. You have been faithful over a few things, I will set you over many things. Enter into the joy of your lord.’"},"matthew-28-20":{"ref":"Matthew 28:20","text":"… Behold, I am with you always, even to the end of the age. Amen."},"mark-11-25":{"ref":"Mark 11:25","text":"Whenever you stand praying, forgive, if you have anything against anyone; so that your Father, who is in heaven, may also forgive you your transgressions."},"luke-6-37":{"ref":"Luke 6:37","text":"Don’t judge, and you won’t be judged. Don’t condemn, and you won’t be condemned. Set free, and you will be set free."},"john-14-1-3":{"ref":"John 14:1-3","text":"Don’t let your heart be troubled. Believe in God. Believe also in me. In my Father’s house are many homes. If it weren’t so, I would have told you. I am going to prepare a place for you. If I go and prepare a place for you, I will come again, and will receive you to myself; that where I am, you may be there also."},"john-14-27":{"ref":"John 14:27","text":"Peace I leave with you. My peace I give to you; not as the world gives, I give to you. Don’t let your heart be troubled, neither let it be fearful."},"john-16-33":{"ref":"John 16:33","text":"I have told you these things, that in me you may have peace. In the world you have trouble; but cheer up! I have overcome the world."},"philippians-4-6-7":{"ref":"Philippians 4:6-7","text":"In nothing be anxious, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God. And the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and your thoughts in Christ Jesus."},"philippians-4-8":{"ref":"Philippians 4:8","text":"Finally, brothers, whatever things are true, whatever things are honorable, whatever things are just, whatever things are pure, whatever things are lovely, whatever things are of good report: if there is any virtue and if there is any praise, think about these things."},"philippians-4-11-12":{"ref":"Philippians 4:11-12","text":"Not that I speak because of lack, for I have learned in whatever state I am, to be content in it. I know how to be humbled, and I also know how to abound. In everything and in all things I have learned the secret both to be filled and to be hungry, both to abound and to be in need."},"philippians-4-13":{"ref":"Philippians 4:13","text":"I can do all things through Christ, who strengthens me."},"philippians-4-19":{"ref":"Philippians 4:19","text":"My God will supply every need of yours according to his riches in glory in Christ Jesus."},"philippians-1-6":{"ref":"Philippians 1:6","text":"being confident of this very thing, that he who began a good work in you will complete it until the day of Jesus Christ."},"philippians-3-13-14":{"ref":"Philippians 3:13-14","text":"Brothers, I don’t regard myself as yet having taken hold, but one thing I do: forgetting the things which are behind, and stretching forward to the things which are before, I press on toward the goal for the prize of the high calling of God in Christ Jesus."},"1-peter-5-7":{"ref":"1 Peter 5:7","text":"casting all your worries on him, because he cares for you."},"1-peter-4-8":{"ref":"1 Peter 4:8","text":"And above all things be earnest in your love among yourselves, for love covers a multitude of sins."},"1-peter-3-9":{"ref":"1 Peter 3:9","text":"not rendering evil for evil, or insult for insult; but instead blessing, knowing that you were called to this, that you may inherit a blessing."},"2-timothy-1-7":{"ref":"2 Timothy 1:7","text":"For God didn’t give us a spirit of fear, but of power, love, and self-control."},"2-timothy-4-7":{"ref":"2 Timothy 4:7","text":"I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith."},"1-timothy-4-12":{"ref":"1 Timothy 4:12","text":"Let no man despise your youth; but be an example to those who believe, in word, in your way of life, in love, in spirit, in faith, and in purity."},"1-timothy-6-6-8":{"ref":"1 Timothy 6:6-8","text":"But godliness with contentment is great gain. For we brought nothing into the world, and we certainly can’t carry anything out. But having food and clothing, we will be content with that."},"james-1-5":{"ref":"James 1:5","text":"But if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach, and it will be given to him."},"james-1-19":{"ref":"James 1:19","text":"So, then, my beloved brothers, let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak, and slow to anger;"},"james-3-17":{"ref":"James 3:17","text":"But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then peaceful, gentle, reasonable, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality, and without hypocrisy."},"james-5-7-8":{"ref":"James 5:7-8","text":"Be patient therefore, brothers, until the coming of the Lord. Behold, the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, being patient over it, until it receives the early and late rain. You also be patient. Establish your hearts, for the coming of the Lord is at hand."},"romans-5-3-5":{"ref":"Romans 5:3-5","text":"Not only this, but we also rejoice in our sufferings, knowing that suffering produces perseverance; and perseverance, proven character; and proven character, hope: and hope doesn’t disappoint us, because God’s love has been poured into our hearts through the Holy Spirit who was given to us."},"romans-8-25":{"ref":"Romans 8:25","text":"But if we hope for that which we don’t see, we wait for it with patience."},"romans-8-26":{"ref":"Romans 8:26","text":"In the same way, the Spirit also helps our weaknesses, for we don’t know how to pray as we ought. But the Spirit himself makes intercession for us with groanings which can’t be uttered."},"romans-8-28":{"ref":"Romans 8:28","text":"We know that all things work together for good for those who love God, for those who are called according to his purpose."},"romans-8-38-39":{"ref":"Romans 8:38-39","text":"For I am persuaded that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present, nor things to come, nor powers, nor height, nor depth, nor any other created thing will be able to separate us from God’s love which is in Christ Jesus our Lord."},"romans-12-12":{"ref":"Romans 12:12","text":"rejoicing in hope; enduring in troubles; continuing steadfastly in prayer;"},"romans-12-17-18":{"ref":"Romans 12:17-18","text":"Repay no one evil for evil. Respect what is honorable in the sight of all men. If it is possible, as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men."},"romans-15-13":{"ref":"Romans 15:13","text":"Now may the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace in believing, that you may abound in hope, in the power of the Holy Spirit."},"1-corinthians-13-4-7":{"ref":"1 Corinthians 13:4-7","text":"Love is patient and is kind. Love doesn’t envy. Love doesn’t brag, is not proud, doesn’t behave itself inappropriately, doesn’t seek its own way, is not provoked, takes no account of evil; doesn’t rejoice in unrighteousness, but rejoices with the truth; bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, and endures all things."},"2-corinthians-1-3-4":{"ref":"2 Corinthians 1:3-4","text":"Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of mercies and God of all comfort; who comforts us in all our affliction, that we may be able to comfort those who are in any affliction, through the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God."},"2-corinthians-5-17":{"ref":"2 Corinthians 5:17","text":"Therefore if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation. The old things have passed away. Behold, all things have become new."},"2-corinthians-12-9":{"ref":"2 Corinthians 12:9","text":"He has said to me, “My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness.” Most gladly therefore I will rather glory in my weaknesses, that the power of Christ may rest on me."},"galatians-5-22-23":{"ref":"Galatians 5:22-23","text":"But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faith, gentleness, and self-control. Against such things there is no law."},"galatians-6-9":{"ref":"Galatians 6:9","text":"Let’s not be weary in doing good, for we will reap in due season, if we don’t give up."},"ephesians-4-2-3":{"ref":"Ephesians 4:2-3","text":"with all lowliness and humility, with patience, bearing with one another in love, being eager to keep the unity of the Spirit in the bond of peace."},"ephesians-4-26":{"ref":"Ephesians 4:26","text":"“Be angry, and don’t sin.” Don’t let the sun go down on your wrath,"},"ephesians-4-32":{"ref":"Ephesians 4:32","text":"And be kind to one another, tender hearted, forgiving each other, just as God also in Christ forgave you."},"colossians-3-13":{"ref":"Colossians 3:13","text":"bearing with one another, and forgiving each other, if any man has a complaint against any; even as Christ forgave you, so you also do."},"colossians-3-14":{"ref":"Colossians 3:14","text":"Above all these things, walk in love, which is the bond of perfection."},"colossians-3-15":{"ref":"Colossians 3:15","text":"And let the peace of God rule in your hearts, to which also you were called in one body, and be thankful."},"colossians-3-23-24":{"ref":"Colossians 3:23-24","text":"And whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men, knowing that from the Lord you will receive the reward of the inheritance; for you serve the Lord Christ."},"hebrews-6-19":{"ref":"Hebrews 6:19","text":"This hope we have as an anchor of the soul, a hope both sure and steadfast and entering into that which is within the veil;"},"hebrews-11-8":{"ref":"Hebrews 11:8","text":"By faith, Abraham, when he was called, obeyed to go out to the place which he was to receive for an inheritance. He went out, not knowing where he went."},"hebrews-13-5":{"ref":"Hebrews 13:5","text":"Be free from the love of money, content with such things as you have, for he has said, “I will in no way leave you, neither will I in any way forsake you.”"},"1-john-1-9":{"ref":"1 John 1:9","text":"If we confess our sins, he is faithful and righteous to forgive us the sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness."},"revelation-21-4":{"ref":"Revelation 21:4","text":"He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more; neither will there be mourning, nor crying, nor pain, any more. The first things have passed away."},"psalm-4-8":{"ref":"Psalm 4:8","text":"In peace I will both lay myself down and sleep, for you, Yahweh alone, make me live in safety."},"psalm-23-1":{"ref":"Psalm 23:1","text":"Yahweh is my shepherd: I shall lack nothing."},"psalm-23-4":{"ref":"Psalm 23:4","text":"Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me. Your rod and your staff, they comfort me."},"psalm-25-4-5":{"ref":"Psalm 25:4-5","text":"Show me your ways, Yahweh. Teach me your paths. Guide me in your truth, and teach me, For you are the God of my salvation, I wait for you all day long."},"psalm-25-16":{"ref":"Psalm 25:16","text":"Turn to me, and have mercy on me, for I am desolate and afflicted."},"psalm-27-1":{"ref":"Psalm 27:1","text":"Yahweh is my light and my salvation. Whom shall I fear? Yahweh is the strength of my life. Of whom shall I be afraid?"},"psalm-27-10":{"ref":"Psalm 27:10","text":"When my father and my mother forsake me, then Yahweh will take me up."},"psalm-27-14":{"ref":"Psalm 27:14","text":"Wait for Yahweh. Be strong, and let your heart take courage. Yes, wait for Yahweh."},"psalm-30-2":{"ref":"Psalm 30:2","text":"Yahweh my God, I cried to you, and you have healed me."},"psalm-32-8":{"ref":"Psalm 32:8","text":"I will instruct you and teach you in the way which you shall go. I will counsel you with my eye on you."},"psalm-34-18":{"ref":"Psalm 34:18","text":"Yahweh is near to those who have a broken heart, and saves those who have a crushed spirit."},"psalm-37-7":{"ref":"Psalm 37:7","text":"Rest in Yahweh, and wait patiently for him. Don’t fret because of him who prospers in his way, because of the man who makes wicked plots happen."},"psalm-37-25":{"ref":"Psalm 37:25","text":"I have been young, and now am old, yet I have not seen the righteous forsaken, nor his children begging for bread."},"psalm-40-1-2":{"ref":"Psalm 40:1-2","text":"I waited patiently for Yahweh. He turned to me, and heard my cry. He brought me up also out of a horrible pit, out of the miry clay. He set my feet on a rock, and gave me a firm place to stand."},"psalm-41-3":{"ref":"Psalm 41:3","text":"Yahweh will sustain him on his sickbed, and restore him from his bed of illness."},"psalm-42-11":{"ref":"Psalm 42:11","text":"Why are you in despair, my soul? Why are you disturbed within me? Hope in God! For I shall still praise him, the saving help of my countenance, and my God."},"psalm-46-1":{"ref":"Psalm 46:1","text":"God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble."},"psalm-46-10":{"ref":"Psalm 46:10","text":"“Be still, and know that I am God. I will be exalted among the nations. I will be exalted in the earth.”"},"psalm-55-22":{"ref":"Psalm 55:22","text":"Cast your burden on Yahweh and he will sustain you. He will never allow the righteous to be moved."},"psalm-56-3":{"ref":"Psalm 56:3","text":"When I am afraid, I will put my trust in you."},"psalm-61-2":{"ref":"Psalm 61:2","text":"From the end of the earth, I will call to you when my heart is overwhelmed. Lead me to the rock that is higher than I."},"psalm-62-1-2":{"ref":"Psalm 62:1-2","text":"My soul rests in God alone. My salvation is from him. He alone is my rock, my salvation, and my fortress. I will never be greatly shaken."},"psalm-63-6":{"ref":"Psalm 63:6","text":"when I remember you on my bed, and think about you in the night watches."},"psalm-68-6":{"ref":"Psalm 68:6","text":"God sets the lonely in families. He brings out the prisoners with singing, but the rebellious dwell in a sun-scorched land."},"psalm-71-18":{"ref":"Psalm 71:18","text":"Yes, even when I am old and gray-haired, God, don’t forsake me, until I have declared your strength to the next generation, your might to everyone who is to come."},"psalm-73-23-24":{"ref":"Psalm 73:23-24","text":"Nevertheless, I am continually with you. You have held my right hand. You will guide me with your counsel, and afterward receive me to glory."},"psalm-73-26":{"ref":"Psalm 73:26","text":"My flesh and my heart fails, but God is the strength of my heart and my portion forever."},"psalm-90-1":{"ref":"Psalm 90:1","text":"Lord, you have been our dwelling place for all generations."},"psalm-90-12":{"ref":"Psalm 90:12","text":"So teach us to count our days, that we may gain a heart of wisdom."},"psalm-90-17":{"ref":"Psalm 90:17","text":"Let the favor of the Lord our God be on us. Establish the work of our hands for us. Yes, establish the work of our hands."},"psalm-91-1":{"ref":"Psalm 91:1","text":"He who dwells in the secret place of the Most High will rest in the shadow of the Almighty."},"psalm-91-11":{"ref":"Psalm 91:11","text":"For he will put his angels in charge of you, to guard you in all your ways."},"psalm-92-14":{"ref":"Psalm 92:14","text":"They will still produce fruit in old age. They will be full of sap and green,"},"psalm-94-19":{"ref":"Psalm 94:19","text":"In the multitude of my thoughts within me, your comforts delight my soul."},"psalm-103-12":{"ref":"Psalm 103:12","text":"As far as the east is from the west, so far has he removed our transgressions from us."},"psalm-112-7":{"ref":"Psalm 112:7","text":"He will not be afraid of evil news. His heart is steadfast, trusting in Yahweh."},"psalm-116-15":{"ref":"Psalm 116:15","text":"Precious in Yahweh’s sight is the death of his saints."},"psalm-118-24":{"ref":"Psalm 118:24","text":"This is the day that Yahweh has made. We will rejoice and be glad in it!"},"psalm-121-1-2":{"ref":"Psalm 121:1-2","text":"I will lift up my eyes to the hills. Where does my help come from? My help comes from Yahweh, who made heaven and earth."},"psalm-121-7-8":{"ref":"Psalm 121:7-8","text":"Yahweh will keep you from all evil. He will keep your soul. Yahweh will keep your going out and your coming in, from this time forward, and forever more."},"psalm-127-1":{"ref":"Psalm 127:1","text":"Unless Yahweh builds the house, they who build it labor in vain. Unless Yahweh watches over the city, the watchman guards it in vain."},"psalm-127-2":{"ref":"Psalm 127:2","text":"It is vain for you to rise up early, to stay up late, eating the bread of toil, for he gives sleep to his loved ones."},"psalm-131-1-2":{"ref":"Psalm 131:1-2","text":"Yahweh, my heart isn’t arrogant, nor my eyes lofty; nor do I concern myself with great matters, or things too wonderful for me. Surely I have stilled and quieted my soul, like a weaned child with his mother, like a weaned child is my soul within me."},"psalm-139-7-8":{"ref":"Psalm 139:7-8","text":"Where could I go from your Spirit? Or where could I flee from your presence? If I ascend up into heaven, you are there. If I make my bed in Sheol, behold, you are there!"},"psalm-139-9-10":{"ref":"Psalm 139:9-10","text":"If I take the wings of the dawn, and settle in the uttermost parts of the sea, even there your hand will lead me, and your right hand will hold me."},"psalm-139-23-24":{"ref":"Psalm 139:23-24","text":"Search me, God, and know my heart. Try me, and know my thoughts. See if there is any wicked way in me, and lead me in the everlasting way."},"psalm-143-8":{"ref":"Psalm 143:8","text":"Cause me to hear your loving kindness in the morning, for I trust in you. Cause me to know the way in which I should walk, for I lift up my soul to you."},"psalm-147-3":{"ref":"Psalm 147:3","text":"He heals the broken in heart, and binds up their wounds."},"proverbs-2-6":{"ref":"Proverbs 2:6","text":"For Yahweh gives wisdom. Out of his mouth comes knowledge and understanding."},"proverbs-3-5-6":{"ref":"Proverbs 3:5-6","text":"Trust in Yahweh with all your heart, and don’t lean on your own understanding. In all your ways acknowledge him, and he will make your paths straight."},"proverbs-3-23-24":{"ref":"Proverbs 3:23-24","text":"Then you shall walk in your way securely. Your foot won’t stumble. When you lie down, you will not be afraid. Yes, you will lie down, and your sleep will be sweet."},"proverbs-4-7":{"ref":"Proverbs 4:7","text":"Wisdom is supreme. Get wisdom. Yes, though it costs all your possessions, get understanding."},"proverbs-9-10":{"ref":"Proverbs 9:10","text":"The fear of Yahweh is the beginning of wisdom. The knowledge of the Holy One is understanding."},"proverbs-14-29":{"ref":"Proverbs 14:29","text":"He who is slow to anger has great understanding, but he who has a quick temper displays folly."},"proverbs-15-1":{"ref":"Proverbs 15:1","text":"A gentle answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger."},"proverbs-15-18":{"ref":"Proverbs 15:18","text":"A wrathful man stirs up contention, but one who is slow to anger appeases strife."},"proverbs-15-22":{"ref":"Proverbs 15:22","text":"Where there is no counsel, plans fail; but in a multitude of counselors they are established."},"proverbs-16-3":{"ref":"Proverbs 16:3","text":"Commit your deeds to Yahweh, and your plans shall succeed."},"proverbs-16-9":{"ref":"Proverbs 16:9","text":"A man’s heart plans his course, but Yahweh directs his steps."},"proverbs-16-31":{"ref":"Proverbs 16:31","text":"Gray hair is a crown of glory. It is attained by a life of righteousness."},"proverbs-16-32":{"ref":"Proverbs 16:32","text":"One who is slow to anger is better than the mighty; one who rules his spirit, than he who takes a city."},"proverbs-17-14":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:14","text":"The beginning of strife is like breaching a dam, therefore stop contention before quarreling breaks out."},"proverbs-17-17":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:17","text":"A friend loves at all times; and a brother is born for adversity."},"proverbs-19-11":{"ref":"Proverbs 19:11","text":"The discretion of a man makes him slow to anger. It is his glory to overlook an offense."},"proverbs-24-3-4":{"ref":"Proverbs 24:3-4","text":"Through wisdom a house is built; by understanding it is established; by knowledge the rooms are filled with all rare and beautiful treasure."},"ecclesiastes-3-1":{"ref":"Ecclesiastes 3:1","text":"For everything there is a season, and a time for every purpose under heaven:"},"ecclesiastes-4-9-10":{"ref":"Ecclesiastes 4:9-10","text":"Two are better than one, because they have a good reward for their labor. For if they fall, the one will lift up his fellow; but woe to him who is alone when he falls, and doesn’t have another to lift him up."},"ecclesiastes-4-12":{"ref":"Ecclesiastes 4:12","text":"If a man prevails against one who is alone, two shall withstand him; and a threefold cord is not quickly broken."},"ecclesiastes-7-8":{"ref":"Ecclesiastes 7:8","text":"Better is the end of a thing than its beginning. The patient in spirit is better than the proud in spirit."},"isaiah-26-3":{"ref":"Isaiah 26:3","text":"You will keep whoever’s mind is steadfast in perfect peace, because he trusts in you."},"isaiah-30-21":{"ref":"Isaiah 30:21","text":"and when you turn to the right hand, and when you turn to the left, your ears will hear a voice behind you, saying, “This is the way. Walk in it.”"},"isaiah-40-29":{"ref":"Isaiah 40:29","text":"He gives power to the weak. He increases the strength of him who has no might."},"isaiah-40-31":{"ref":"Isaiah 40:31","text":"but those who wait for Yahweh will renew their strength. They will mount up with wings like eagles. They will run, and not be weary. They will walk, and not faint."},"isaiah-41-10":{"ref":"Isaiah 41:10","text":"Don’t you be afraid, for I am with you. Don’t be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you. Yes, I will help you. Yes, I will uphold you with the right hand of my righteousness."},"isaiah-43-2":{"ref":"Isaiah 43:2","text":"When you pass through the waters, I will be with you, and through the rivers, they will not overflow you. When you walk through the fire, you will not be burned, and flame will not scorch you."},"isaiah-43-18-19":{"ref":"Isaiah 43:18-19","text":"Don’t remember the former things, and don’t consider the things of old. Behold, I will do a new thing. It springs out now. Don’t you know it? I will even make a way in the wilderness, and rivers in the desert."},"isaiah-46-4":{"ref":"Isaiah 46:4","text":"Even to old age I am he, and even to gray hairs I will carry you. I have made, and I will bear. Yes, I will carry, and will deliver."},"jeremiah-17-14":{"ref":"Jeremiah 17:14","text":"Heal me, O Yahweh, and I will be healed. Save me, and I will be saved; for you are my praise."},"jeremiah-29-11":{"ref":"Jeremiah 29:11","text":"For I know the thoughts that I think toward you,” says Yahweh, “thoughts of peace, and not of evil, to give you hope and a future."},"lamentations-3-22-23":{"ref":"Lamentations 3:22-23","text":"It is because of Yahweh’s loving kindnesses that we are not consumed, because his compassion doesn’t fail. They are new every morning. Great is your faithfulness."},"lamentations-3-25-26":{"ref":"Lamentations 3:25-26","text":"Yahweh is good to those who wait for him, to the soul who seeks him. It is good that a man should hope and quietly wait for the salvation of Yahweh."},"habakkuk-2-3":{"ref":"Habakkuk 2:3","text":"For the vision is yet for the appointed time, and it hurries toward the end, and won’t prove false. Though it takes time, wait for it; because it will surely come. It won’t delay."},"micah-6-8":{"ref":"Micah 6:8","text":"He has shown you, O man, what is good. What does Yahweh require of you, but to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God?"},"micah-7-7":{"ref":"Micah 7:7","text":"But as for me, I will look to Yahweh. I will wait for the God of my salvation. My God will hear me."},"genesis-12-1":{"ref":"Genesis 12:1","text":"Now Yahweh said to Abram, Leave your country, and your relatives, and your father’s house, and go to the land that I will show you."},"genesis-28-15":{"ref":"Genesis 28:15","text":"Behold, I am with you, and will keep you, wherever you go, and will bring you again into this land. For I will not leave you, until I have done that which I have spoken of to you."},"exodus-4-12":{"ref":"Exodus 4:12","text":"Now therefore go, and I will be with your mouth, and teach you what you shall speak."},"exodus-14-14":{"ref":"Exodus 14:14","text":"Yahweh will fight for you, and you shall be still."},"exodus-33-14":{"ref":"Exodus 33:14","text":"He said, “My presence will go with you, and I will give you rest.”"},"numbers-6-24-26":{"ref":"Numbers 6:24-26","text":"‘Yahweh bless you, and keep you. Yahweh make his face to shine on you, and be gracious to you. Yahweh lift up his face toward you, and give you peace.’"},"deuteronomy-31-6":{"ref":"Deuteronomy 31:6","text":"Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid or scared of them; for Yahweh your God himself is who goes with you. He will not fail you nor forsake you."},"deuteronomy-31-8":{"ref":"Deuteronomy 31:8","text":"Yahweh himself is who goes before you. He will be with you. He will not fail you nor forsake you. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be discouraged."},"joshua-1-9":{"ref":"Joshua 1:9","text":"Haven’t I commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid. Don’t be dismayed, for Yahweh your God is with you wherever you go."},"john-11-35":{"ref":"John 11:35","text":"Jesus wept."},"psalm-30-5":{"ref":"Psalm 30:5","text":"For his anger is but for a moment. His favor is for a lifetime. Weeping may stay for the night, but joy comes in the morning."},"psalm-56-8":{"ref":"Psalm 56:8","text":"You count my wanderings. You put my tears into your container. Aren’t they in your book?"},"john-11-25-26":{"ref":"John 11:25-26","text":"Jesus said to her, “I am the resurrection and the life. He who believes in me will still live, even if he dies. Whoever lives and believes in me will never die. Do you believe this?”","jesus":"partial","jesusFrom":"I am the resurrection and the life"},"1-thessalonians-4-13":{"ref":"1 Thessalonians 4:13","text":"But we don’t want you to be ignorant, brothers, concerning those who have fallen asleep, so that you don’t grieve like the rest, who have no hope."}},"redLetter":true,"pages":[{"slug":"worry","url":"/topics/worry/","title":"Bible Verses for Worry","h1":"Bible verses for worry","label":"Worry","for":"worry","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["worry","worried","worrying","worries","nervous","stress","stressed","stressing","uneasy","concerned","fretting","what if","tomorrow","i can't stop thinking","cant stop thinking","overthinking","overthink","racing thoughts"],"verses":[{"id":"matthew-6-34","why":"Jesus doesn't claim tomorrow will be easy. The point is narrower: tomorrow will bring its own concerns, and carrying them today only doubles the load. This is the last line of a longer section in Matthew 6 about birds, flowers, and a Father who already knows what you need. Read the whole stretch and the theme is trust, not denial."},{"id":"philippians-4-6-7","why":"Paul wrote this from custody, to a church he loved, so it isn't advice from a comfortable distance. The instruction is concrete: turn each worry into a specific request, and add thanks. The promise that follows isn't that the problem disappears but that peace 'guards' the heart, a military word for a sentry at a gate."},{"id":"1-peter-5-7","why":"One short sentence with a reason attached: because he cares for you. Peter is writing to scattered, pressured Christians, and the verse before it is about humility. Worry often hides a quiet assumption that everything depends on us. This line pushes back on that assumption gently."},{"id":"matthew-6-26","why":"Birds don't plan, and they're fed anyway. Jesus isn't telling anyone to stop working; he's asking a question about worth. If God provides for creatures that can't plan at all, the anxious math about your own future is missing a variable."},{"id":"psalm-55-22","why":"David wrote this psalm while being betrayed by a close friend, which is worth knowing. 'Cast your burden' is not a tidy religious phrase here; it's what you say when the situation is real and you're out of options. The psalm promises sustaining, not that the betrayal reverses."},{"id":"isaiah-26-3","why":"The peace described here is tied to a fixed mind: attention that keeps returning to God rather than circling the problem. It's part of a song about a secure city. Worry is often exactly the opposite, a mind that won't stay anywhere. The verse names the alternative without shaming the struggle."},{"id":"psalm-94-19","why":"This is one of the more honest lines in the Psalms about an overactive mind. The writer doesn't claim the thoughts stop. He says that in the middle of them, comfort arrives. If your worry shows up as a crowd of competing thoughts, this verse describes your situation accurately."}],"related":["anxiety","peace","waiting","cannot-sleep","financial-stress","overwhelmed"]},{"slug":"anxiety","url":"/topics/anxiety/","title":"Bible Verses for Anxiety","h1":"Bible verses for anxiety","label":"Anxiety","for":"anxiety","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["anxiety","anxious","panic","panic attack","fear","afraid","scared","terrified","frightened","dread","on edge","can't breathe","cant breathe","heart racing"],"verses":[{"id":"isaiah-41-10","why":"Five short promises in one verse: I'm with you, I'm your God, I'll strengthen you, I'll help you, I'll hold you up. It's addressed to Israel in exile, people who had real reasons for fear. The verse doesn't argue with the fear. It answers it with presence, which is what anxious people usually need first."},{"id":"philippians-4-6-7","why":"Notice the sequence. Anxiety, then prayer with specific requests, then thanksgiving, then a peace that doesn't make logical sense ('surpasses all understanding'). Paul isn't offering a technique so much as describing what happens when the anxious loop gets interrupted by turning outward."},{"id":"psalm-56-3","why":"Not 'I won't be afraid' but 'when I am afraid.' David wrote this after being captured by enemies, so the fear was warranted. The verse assumes fear will come and describes what to do in the middle of it. That's more useful than pretending it won't."},{"id":"2-timothy-1-7","why":"Paul is writing to a younger leader who seems to have been timid under pressure. The verse contrasts a spirit of fear with three things: power, love, and self-control (or a sound mind). For anxiety that feels like losing control of your own thoughts, that last word is the one to hold onto."},{"id":"john-14-27","why":"Jesus says this the night before his arrest, to friends who are about to be terrified. The peace he offers is explicitly 'not as the world gives,' which usually means removing the threat. His is a peace that can exist while the threat is still there."},{"id":"psalm-46-1","why":"The whole psalm is about the ground shaking and God staying put. 'Very present' means near, available now, not eventually. For an anxious moment, the picture of a refuge you can step into immediately is the point. The psalm goes on to picture mountains falling into the sea and nations in uproar, then answers with 'Be still, and know that I am God.' It was written for people whose world felt unstable, which is roughly what anxiety feels like from the inside."},{"id":"1-peter-5-7","why":"The Greek word for 'worries' here can also mean 'cares' or 'distractions.' Peter is telling scattered, persecuted believers to hand the whole pile over, and the reason he gives is simply that God cares. Anxiety often carries an unspoken belief that no one else is holding this. This verse says otherwise."}],"related":["worry","peace","cannot-sleep","overwhelmed","waiting-for-test-results","courage"]},{"slug":"courage","url":"/topics/courage/","title":"Bible Verses for Courage","h1":"Bible verses for courage","label":"Courage","for":"courage","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["courage","brave","bravery","be strong","strength","strong","confidence","confident","boldness","bold","nerve","face something","hard conversation","stand up"],"verses":[{"id":"joshua-1-9","why":"Joshua has just inherited leadership from Moses, the only leader the nation has ever known, and is about to cross into hostile territory. God says 'be courageous' three times in nine verses. The command comes with a reason: 'Yahweh your God is with you wherever you go.' The courage rests on the company, not on Joshua's nerve."},{"id":"deuteronomy-31-6","why":"Moses, at the end of his life, speaking to the whole people before they go on without him. 'He will not fail you nor forsake you' is the promise the New Testament later quotes in Hebrews 13. If courage feels impossible because you're losing someone or something you leaned on, this is the passage."},{"id":"psalm-27-1","why":"Two rhetorical questions, and the psalm expects the answer 'no one.' David wrote this while surrounded by enemies (verse 3 mentions an army camped against him). The courage is derived: if God is the light and the stronghold, the list of things worth fearing gets short."},{"id":"isaiah-41-10","why":"'Don't be dismayed' is stronger than 'don't worry.' It means don't look around anxiously for help. The verse answers with a hand: 'I will uphold you with the right hand of my righteousness.' For a moment that needs steadiness more than boldness, this is the verse."},{"id":"2-timothy-1-7","why":"Timothy seems to have been naturally cautious, and Paul doesn't scold him for it. He reminds him what he's been given: power, love, and a sound mind. Courage in this verse looks like clear thinking and love, not bravado. 'Fear' here is the word for cowardice or timidity, a shrinking back, not the healthy caution that keeps you alive. Paul writes it from prison, to a young pastor who is watching his mentor suffer, so it isn't cheap advice."},{"id":"psalm-46-1","why":"Written for a city under threat, this psalm describes mountains falling into the sea and says, essentially, we still won't fear. It's the source of Luther's 'A Mighty Fortress.' The courage isn't naive; the psalm names the chaos and then names something stronger."},{"id":"exodus-14-14","why":"Israel is trapped between Pharaoh's army and the sea. Moses' instruction isn't 'fight bravely' but 'be still.' Sometimes courage is doing the hard thing; sometimes it's not panicking and letting God act. Knowing which situation you're in matters. The next verse has God telling Moses to stop crying out and get the people moving, so stillness and action sit side by side in the same story. Courage may mean waiting quietly for the sea to open, and then walking."}],"related":["hope","anxiety","job-interview","starting-over","before-surgery","difficult-decisions"]},{"slug":"hope","url":"/topics/hope/","title":"Bible Verses for Hope","h1":"Bible verses for hope","label":"Hope","for":"hope","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["hope","hopeless","hopeful","discouraged","discouragement","despair","giving up","give up","want to give up","lost hope","nothing is working","feel stuck","stuck","future","encouragement","encourage","down","feeling down","sad","sadness","depressed"],"verses":[{"id":"romans-15-13","why":"Paul calls God 'the God of hope,' as if hope is his characteristic gift. The prayer is that you'd 'abound' in it, overflow, not scrape by. Notice hope here is tied to joy and peace 'in believing,' which means it grows with trust rather than with better circumstances."},{"id":"lamentations-3-22-23","why":"Lamentations is a book of grief written after Jerusalem was destroyed. These verses sit in the exact middle of it. 'New every morning' isn't a cheerful slogan here; it's what a man says when he's lost almost everything and still finds mercy waiting the next day. That context is what gives the line its weight."},{"id":"jeremiah-29-11","why":"Often printed on graduation cards, this verse was actually written to exiles being told they'd stay in Babylon for seventy years. The 'hope and a future' was real, but it wasn't quick. If you're in a long season, that's not a disappointment; it's the verse's original setting."},{"id":"romans-5-3-5","why":"A chain: suffering produces perseverance, perseverance produces character, character produces hope. Hope comes at the end of the process, not the beginning. And 'hope doesn't disappoint' because it rests on God's love already given, not on a predicted outcome. Paul isn't saying suffering is good. He's saying it isn't wasted, because of what it produces in a person over time. The verse ends with God's love 'poured out,' which is where the hope actually rests."},{"id":"psalm-42-11","why":"The psalmist talks to himself: 'Why are you in despair, my soul?' Then he gives himself an instruction: hope in God. This is the third time the refrain appears in Psalms 42 and 43. Hope in the Psalms is sometimes a decision you have to make more than once in the same day."},{"id":"hebrews-6-19","why":"Hope as an anchor, the only place the Bible uses that image. An anchor doesn't stop the storm; it keeps the boat from drifting. The verse says this hope reaches 'within the veil,' into God's own presence, which is why it holds."},{"id":"isaiah-40-31","why":"The chapter opens with 'Comfort, comfort my people' and is addressed to exiles who thought God had forgotten them. 'Those who wait' means those who keep hoping when nothing has changed yet. The strength renews in the waiting, not after it."}],"related":["grief","waiting","overwhelmed","starting-over","job-loss","patience"]},{"slug":"peace","url":"/topics/peace/","title":"Bible Verses for Peace","h1":"Bible verses for peace","label":"Peace","for":"peace","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["peace","peaceful","calm","calm down","rest","quiet","settle","unsettled","restless","chaos","chaotic","troubled","my mind won't stop","need to relax"],"verses":[{"id":"john-14-27","why":"Spoken by Jesus hours before his arrest, to disciples who are about to scatter. He 'leaves' peace like a bequest and specifies it's not the world's kind, which depends on circumstances improving. Then two instructions: don't let your heart be troubled, don't let it be afraid. The peace and the fear are both real, and he's addressing both."},{"id":"philippians-4-6-7","why":"The peace here 'surpasses all understanding,' which is Paul's way of saying it won't make sense on paper. It arrives after specific prayer with thanksgiving, and it guards, like a soldier posted at the door of your heart and mind. If peace feels like something you have to manufacture, this passage describes it as something that arrives."},{"id":"isaiah-26-3","why":"'Perfect peace' in Hebrew is literally 'shalom shalom,' peace doubled. It's promised to the person whose mind is steadfast, fixed, leaning on God. This is part of a song of trust sung in Judah. The condition isn't a calm life; it's a mind that keeps returning to the same place."},{"id":"john-16-33","why":"Jesus states the problem plainly ('in the world you have trouble') and doesn't soften it. The peace is 'in me,' located in a person rather than a situation. And 'cheer up' or 'take heart' is a command with a reason: the trouble has already been overcome."},{"id":"psalm-4-8","why":"A bedtime psalm. David lies down and sleeps because God alone makes him live in safety. The earlier verses show he's under pressure from people spreading lies about him. Peace here is the ability to stop being on guard and rest."},{"id":"colossians-3-15","why":"'Let the peace of God rule' uses a word for an umpire, the one who makes the call. Paul is writing about relationships in a church, so this peace isn't purely private; it's meant to settle disputes and be shared. The verse ends 'be thankful,' which shows up next to peace more than once in Paul's letters."},{"id":"numbers-6-24-26","why":"The oldest blessing in the Bible, given to Aaron to speak over the people. It ends with peace. God's face 'shining' on someone and being 'lifted up' toward them are pictures of favor and attention. People still use these words at the end of services and in cards because they've never needed updating."}],"related":["grief","worry","anxiety","cannot-sleep","overwhelmed","waiting"]},{"slug":"patience","url":"/topics/patience/","title":"Bible Verses for Patience","h1":"Bible verses for patience","label":"Patience","for":"patience","cluster":"decisions","aliases":["patience","patient","impatient","impatience","temper","losing my temper","annoyed","irritated","frustrated","frustration","anger","angry","mad","furious"],"verses":[{"id":"james-5-7-8","why":"James uses a farmer who's planted and now can't do anything but wait for rain. The waiting isn't passive; he's 'patient over it,' attentive. 'Establish your hearts' means settle them, stop them swinging. For a season where you've done what you can and now have to wait, this is the picture."},{"id":"galatians-5-22-23","why":"Patience is listed among the fruit of the Spirit, which matters: fruit grows, it isn't manufactured. Paul places it between peace and kindness. If you're trying to force patience by gritting your teeth, this verse suggests a different approach, one that treats it as something produced in you over time."},{"id":"proverbs-14-29","why":"A proverb contrasts two people: the slow-to-anger person has great understanding, and the quick-tempered one puts foolishness on display. The point isn't that anger is always wrong but that speed is the problem. Patience here is mostly about the gap between feeling and reacting."},{"id":"proverbs-16-32","why":"A person who rules their own temper is compared favorably to a general capturing a city. In the ancient world that's about the highest praise available. If patience feels unimpressive next to action, this proverb ranks it higher. The pairing is deliberate: 'slow to anger' and 'rules his spirit' are one skill, seen from the inside and the outside. It takes years to build, like a city takes years to take. Proverbs treats it as the more lasting conquest."},{"id":"ephesians-4-2-3","why":"'Bearing with one another in love' is patience applied to specific people you're stuck with. Paul writes it to a church trying to stay unified across real differences. The verse doesn't say pretend the friction isn't there; it says carry it, for the sake of keeping peace."},{"id":"ecclesiastes-7-8","why":"'The patient in spirit is better than the proud in spirit' pairs patience with humility and impatience with pride, which is often accurate. And 'the end of a thing is better than its beginning' is a reminder that how something finishes matters more than how it started."},{"id":"romans-8-25","why":"Paul defines patience as waiting for what you don't see yet. That's the whole difficulty. If you could see it, you wouldn't need patience. The verse comes in a passage about creation itself groaning and waiting, which puts your own waiting in a very large context."}],"related":["waiting","difficult-coworkers","difficult-family-members","hope","peace","forgiveness"]},{"slug":"wisdom","url":"/topics/wisdom/","title":"Bible Verses for Wisdom","h1":"Bible verses for wisdom","label":"Wisdom","for":"wisdom","cluster":"decisions","aliases":["wisdom","wise","discernment","discern","judgment","understanding","clarity","clear thinking","advice","guidance","insight"],"verses":[{"id":"james-1-5","why":"The most direct verse on the subject: if you lack wisdom, ask. Two details matter. God gives 'liberally,' generously, and 'without reproach,' without making you feel foolish for asking. James writes this to people going through trials, so wisdom here means knowing how to handle hard things, not abstract knowledge."},{"id":"proverbs-3-5-6","why":"'Don't lean on your own understanding' isn't anti-thinking; Proverbs is a whole book about thinking well. It's a warning against treating your own view as the final word. 'He will make your paths straight' is the promise for someone who keeps God in the decision rather than consulting him afterward."},{"id":"proverbs-2-6","why":"Wisdom has a source. The chapter describes searching for it like silver, then says plainly it comes from God's mouth. That's why prayer and wisdom sit together throughout the Bible. Effort matters, but the origin is a gift. The verse sits between two ideas: the chapter opens with 'if you cry out for discernment' and then says wisdom comes from God's mouth. So the effort and the gift go together. Ask and look at the same time."},{"id":"james-3-17","why":"James gives a checklist for real wisdom: pure, peaceful, gentle, reasonable, merciful, impartial, sincere. It's useful for testing advice, including your own. If a 'wise' course of action is harsh, self-serving, or divisive, this verse says it fails the test."},{"id":"proverbs-15-22","why":"Plans fail without counsel and succeed with many advisers. This is wisdom as a social practice: ask people. Proverbs repeats this idea several times, which suggests the writers knew how tempting it is to decide alone. The counsel in view isn't a crowd shouting opinions; 'advisers' in Proverbs are people with experience who will tell you the truth. Two or three of those are worth more than a poll, and far more than deciding alone."},{"id":"proverbs-9-10","why":"'The fear of Yahweh is the beginning of wisdom' doesn't mean being scared. It means taking God seriously as the fixed point everything else is measured against. Wisdom that starts anywhere else, the proverb suggests, is built on sand. Chapter 9 sets two invitations side by side, from Wisdom and from Folly, using nearly identical words. Both sound appealing. The difference is where each one leads, which is why the starting point matters so much."},{"id":"psalm-90-12","why":"Moses asks God to teach us to count our days, to remember they're limited. The result is 'a heart of wisdom.' Knowing your time is finite changes what seems worth doing. It's one of the oldest and plainest ways the Bible connects wisdom to mortality."}],"related":["difficult-decisions","patience","waiting","job-interview","work-stress","graduation"]},{"slug":"loneliness","url":"/topics/loneliness/","title":"Bible Verses for Loneliness","h1":"Bible verses for loneliness","label":"Loneliness","for":"loneliness","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["loneliness","lonely","alone","all alone","no one","nobody","isolated","isolation","abandoned","left out","forgotten","no friends","by myself","single"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-25-16","why":"'I am desolate and afflicted' is about as plain as the Psalms get. David doesn't dress it up. He asks God to turn toward him, which is a physical picture: someone facing you instead of facing away. If you need words for the feeling itself, this verse gives them."},{"id":"deuteronomy-31-6","why":"'He will not fail you nor forsake you' is said to a people about to lose the only leader they've known. Loneliness often follows a loss like that. The promise isn't that someone will replace what's gone; it's that God himself goes with you into the unfamiliar."},{"id":"psalm-68-6","why":"'God sets the lonely in families' is a line about God's character: he notices the solitary and puts them somewhere they belong. It's descriptive, not a timeline. But it tells you what God is like, which matters when loneliness suggests nobody is paying attention."},{"id":"matthew-28-20","why":"The last words of Matthew's Gospel. Jesus is sending his friends out into the world, and his final promise is presence: 'always, even to the end of the age.' It's not conditional on their success or their mood. If you've felt left behind, this is the opposite."},{"id":"psalm-139-7-8","why":"The psalmist tries to imagine a place God isn't and can't find one, not heaven, not the grave. It's an unusual comfort: there is no location, including the low one you're in, where you'd be out of reach. This psalm is often read at 3 a.m. for a reason."},{"id":"psalm-27-10","why":"'When my father and my mother forsake me' names the deepest kind of abandonment, and then says God takes up the one who's been left. If your loneliness has a family shape to it, this verse doesn't minimize that. It answers it."},{"id":"hebrews-13-5","why":"The writer quotes God's promise from Deuteronomy, 'I will in no way leave you,' with emphatic double negatives in the original. It's set, interestingly, in a verse about contentment and money. Loneliness and the feeling of not having enough often travel together; this verse addresses both."}],"related":["grief","sympathy-card","cannot-sleep","overwhelmed","hope","moving"]},{"slug":"grief","url":"/topics/grief/","title":"Bible Verses for Grief","h1":"Bible verses for grief","label":"Grief","for":"grief","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["grief","grieving","grieve","mourning","mourn","loss","lost my","lost someone","someone died","my mom died","my dad died","my husband died","my wife died","my friend died","passed away","death","died","bereaved","bereavement","miscarriage","funeral","widow","widower","missing someone","heartbroken","heartbreak"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-34-18","why":"David wrote this psalm after a humiliating escape, pretending to be insane to get out of an enemy court. It isn’t a triumphant psalm; it’s the writing of someone who has been reduced. ‘Near to those who have a broken heart’ doesn’t promise the heart will be fixed on a timetable. It says the nearness is a response to the breaking, not to recovery from it. That’s why this is the verse to start with: it meets you where grief actually puts you, on the floor, and says that is exactly where God comes close."},{"id":"john-11-35","why":"The shortest verse in the Bible, and it happens minutes before Jesus raises Lazarus. He knows what he’s about to do, and he weeps anyway, because Mary is weeping and the loss is real. The verse gives you permission that no explanation could: if Jesus grieved at a tomb he was about to empty, grief is not a lack of faith. It’s the correct response to loss. You don’t have to reason your way out of it before you’re allowed to feel it."},{"id":"matthew-5-4","why":"The second Beatitude, and an odd one. Jesus calls mourners blessed, not because grief is pleasant but because of what is promised to them: comfort. Notice he doesn’t say when. The sentence holds the sorrow and the comfort together without collapsing one into the other. Read it slowly on a day when the loss is loud. It doesn’t tell you to stop mourning; it tells you what mourning is headed toward."},{"id":"psalm-30-5","why":"David wrote this looking back on a season he thought he wouldn’t survive. ‘Weeping may stay for the night’ is honest that the night is real and can be long; the Hebrew pictures weeping as a guest who lodges overnight. The joy that comes in the morning isn’t a promise that morning comes tomorrow. It’s the testimony of someone who has been through the night and is telling you it does end. That’s a different kind of comfort than being told to cheer up."},{"id":"2-corinthians-1-3-4","why":"Paul opens this letter after a stretch he describes a few verses later as so hard he despaired of life itself. So ‘the God of all comfort’ isn’t a greeting-card phrase; it comes from a man who needed comforting and got it. The verse also says what comfort is for: so that we can comfort others. Grief connects you to everyone who has grieved. Eventually, though not yet, yours may be what someone else needs."},{"id":"psalm-56-8","why":"‘You put my tears into your container’ is a strange, tender image. In the ancient world, mourners sometimes kept tears in small bottles. The psalmist imagines God doing that: collecting each one, writing it down, missing none. On the days grief feels invisible to everyone around you, this verse says it is fully seen. Not fixed, not explained. Counted."},{"id":"john-11-25-26","why":"Spoken to Martha at her brother’s tomb, before anything has changed. Jesus doesn’t say ‘I will do a resurrection’; he says ‘I am the resurrection,’ and then asks her, with the loss still fresh, whether she believes it. It’s the most direct thing the Bible says about death, and it’s said to a grieving woman, not a classroom. If you can’t answer Martha’s question yet, that’s allowed. She said yes with tears on her face."},{"id":"1-thessalonians-4-13","why":"Paul is writing to a young church whose members have started dying, and the survivors don’t know what to think. His line is careful: not ‘don’t grieve’ but ‘don’t grieve like those who have no hope.’ Grief is assumed. Hope changes its shape, not its existence. This is the verse for a Christian who feels guilty for being devastated. Paul expected you to be."},{"id":"revelation-21-4","why":"The end of the story. John’s vision of the new creation ends with a hand wiping away tears, and a list of things that will be gone: death, mourning, crying, pain. The verse only means something because those things are real now. It’s a promise for later, and it’s honest about that. On the worst days, some people find it helps to know the last page has already been written."}],"related":["sympathy-card","loneliness","peace","hope","cannot-sleep","overwhelmed"]},{"slug":"overwhelmed","url":"/topics/overwhelmed/","title":"Bible Verses for Feeling Overwhelmed","h1":"Bible verses for feeling overwhelmed","label":"Overwhelmed","for":"when you're overwhelmed","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["overwhelmed","overwhelming","too much","burned out","burnout","burnt out","exhausted","exhaustion","tired","worn out","drained","can't keep up","cant keep up","falling apart","breaking down","drowning","weak","weakness","weary","burden","heavy"],"verses":[{"id":"matthew-11-28-29","why":"Jesus invites the exhausted specifically: 'all you who labor and are heavily burdened.' He doesn't offer a lighter schedule; he offers rest and a different yoke, one carried alongside him. 'Gentle and humble in heart' is how he describes himself, which is a relief if the voice in your head driving you is neither."},{"id":"psalm-61-2","why":"'When my heart is overwhelmed' is the phrase, and it's in the Bible, from the end of the earth, from someone far from where he wants to be. He asks to be led to a rock higher than himself, a place to stand that he couldn't climb to alone. That's the request when you can't."},{"id":"2-corinthians-12-9","why":"Paul asked three times for a hardship to be removed, and the answer was no, with this sentence attached: 'My grace is sufficient for you, for my power is made perfect in weakness.' Being overwhelmed is weakness, and this verse says weakness is where the strength shows up. It's the opposite of the advice to try harder."},{"id":"psalm-55-22","why":"Cast the burden, and 'he will sustain you.' The word for burden can mean what's been given to you, your lot. David wrote this during a betrayal. The promise is holding up under it, not that the lot changes overnight."},{"id":"isaiah-40-29","why":"'He gives power to the weak.' Two verses later comes the famous 'wings like eagles' line, but it starts here, with an admission of having no might left. The chapter is addressed to people who think God has stopped noticing them. It's the right chapter for running on empty."},{"id":"exodus-14-14","why":"Trapped between an army and the sea, the people panic. Moses says God will fight, and 'you shall be still.' Sometimes the overwhelming thing is that you think every outcome depends on your effort. This verse describes a situation where the outcome didn't."},{"id":"psalm-62-1-2","why":"'My soul rests in God alone.' The word 'alone' does a lot of work: not in God plus a plan, not in God once things calm down. The result is 'I will never be greatly shaken,' which is honest, since 'greatly' admits some shaking still happens."}],"related":["grief","work-stress","anxiety","peace","cannot-sleep","loneliness"]},{"slug":"forgiveness","url":"/topics/forgiveness/","title":"Bible Verses for Forgiveness","h1":"Bible verses for forgiveness","label":"Forgiveness","for":"forgiveness","cluster":"family","aliases":["forgiveness","forgive","forgiving","forgiven","grudge","resentment","resent","bitter","bitterness","guilt","guilty","ashamed","shame","hurt me","betrayed","betrayal","let go"],"verses":[{"id":"ephesians-4-32","why":"The standard for forgiving others is stated in one clause: 'just as God also in Christ forgave you.' That's both the measure and the motive. Paul frames it with kindness and tenderheartedness, which suggests forgiveness is less a legal decision than a softening."},{"id":"colossians-3-13","why":"'Bearing with' and 'forgiving' are two different things here. One is tolerating ongoing friction; the other is releasing a specific complaint. The verse assumes both will be necessary in any real community. 'If any man has a complaint against any' is refreshingly realistic."},{"id":"matthew-18-21-22","why":"Peter thinks seven times is generous. Jesus says seventy times seven, which isn't a number to count to; it's a way of saying stop counting. The parable that follows, about a servant forgiven a huge debt who then refuses to forgive a small one, is the real point of the passage. Read it."},{"id":"matthew-6-14-15","why":"This comes right after the Lord's Prayer and is the one line Jesus stops to expand. The link between being forgiven and forgiving is stated bluntly. It's uncomfortable, and it's meant to be. Jesus doesn't treat refusing to forgive as a minor matter."},{"id":"psalm-103-12","why":"East and west never meet, so the distance is infinite. That's how far God removes our sins. If your need is to know you've been forgiven rather than to forgive someone else, this is the verse. The whole psalm is worth reading for its description of God's character."},{"id":"1-john-1-9","why":"The condition is confession, and the promise is two things: forgiveness and cleansing. God is called 'faithful and righteous' to do this, meaning it's consistent with who he is, not a reluctant exception. This is the verse for guilt that keeps circling back."},{"id":"mark-11-25","why":"Jesus connects forgiveness to prayer: 'whenever you stand praying, forgive.' It implies forgiveness is a recurring act, something you do each time the grudge resurfaces, not a single event. That's more realistic than the idea that you forgive once and it's done."}],"related":["loneliness","difficult-family-members","difficult-coworkers","patience","marriage","peace"]},{"slug":"financial-stress","url":"/topics/financial-stress/","title":"Bible Verses for Financial Stress","h1":"Bible verses for financial stress","label":"Financial stress","for":"financial stress","cluster":"money","aliases":["money","finances","financial","bills","debt","broke","rent","mortgage","can't afford","cant afford","paycheck","budget","provision","provide","poor","poverty","income","contentment","content","enough","worried about money","money worries","stressed about money","anxious about money","financial worry","financial anxiety","lost my job money"],"verses":[{"id":"matthew-6-33","why":"This comes in the middle of Jesus' teaching about not worrying over food and clothes. 'All these things' are the basics, not luxuries, and the promise is that they'll be 'given.' The instruction is about priority: seek the kingdom first and let provision follow. It's not a formula for wealth; it's a reordering."},{"id":"philippians-4-19","why":"Paul says this to a church that had just sent him money while he was in prison. God supplies 'every need' according to 'his riches,' not according to your budget. The verse is about need, and the context is generosity. Both matter for reading it honestly."},{"id":"hebrews-13-5","why":"Contentment is tied directly to God's promise never to leave. The logic: if he's with you, you already have the thing money can't secure. 'Content with such things as you have' is a hard sentence when you're short, but it's addressed to people who were, in fact, short."},{"id":"matthew-6-26","why":"Birds don't plan and are fed anyway. Jesus asks whether you're worth more than they are. The verse doesn't say don't work; it says don't let provision anxiety run your life. It's hard to read this and keep believing everything depends on you."},{"id":"philippians-4-11-12","why":"Paul says he's 'learned' contentment, which means it wasn't automatic. He'd known both plenty and hunger and found a secret that worked in both. The next verse, 'I can do all things through Christ,' is about this: enduring lack, not achieving goals."},{"id":"1-timothy-6-6-8","why":"'Godliness with contentment is great gain.' Paul then reduces the necessities to food and clothing. That's stark, but for someone stressed about money, it's clarifying: the list of what you truly need is shorter than the list of what you're afraid of losing."},{"id":"psalm-37-25","why":"An old man's observation from a long life: he hasn't seen the righteous forsaken. It's testimony, not a guarantee, and it's honest about being one person's experience. The whole psalm is about not fretting over people who seem to prosper unfairly, which is often part of money stress."}],"related":["worry","job-loss","peace","anxiety","waiting","retirement"]},{"slug":"difficult-decisions","url":"/situations/difficult-decisions/","title":"Bible Verses for Making a Difficult Decision","h1":"Bible verses for making a difficult decision","label":"Difficult decisions","for":"a difficult decision","cluster":"decisions","aliases":["decision","decisions","decide","deciding","choice","choose","choosing","crossroads","which way","what should i do","don't know what to do","dont know what to do","direction","path","next step","big decision","hard decision","difficult decision","career","career change","job offer","should i"],"verses":[{"id":"proverbs-3-5-6","why":"The instruction is to bring God into the deciding ('in all your ways acknowledge him') rather than to skip the thinking. 'Don't lean on your own understanding' means don't treat your own read of the situation as the last word. The promise is straight paths, not a map handed over in advance."},{"id":"james-1-5","why":"If you lack wisdom, ask. God gives 'liberally and without reproach,' generously, and without making you feel foolish for not already knowing. It's the most direct verse in the Bible for someone stuck at a fork. The next verse adds 'ask in faith, without any doubting,' which isn't a demand for certainty about the answer. It's about trusting that God is willing to help. Doubt about the decision is normal; doubt about God's willingness is what James addresses."},{"id":"proverbs-15-22","why":"Plans fail without counsel. This is wisdom's most practical instruction: talk to people. Not one person who'll agree with you, but 'a multitude,' several perspectives. Hard decisions made in isolation tend to be harder than they need to be. It also matters who counts as counsel. Proverbs assumes advisers are people with experience who will tell you what you don't want to hear. A friend who already agrees with you is company, not counsel."},{"id":"psalm-32-8","why":"'I will counsel you with my eye on you' is God speaking. The picture is of someone watching closely and guiding, not shouting directions from far off. For a decision where you feel unwatched and on your own, this verse says otherwise."},{"id":"proverbs-16-9","why":"You plan; God directs the steps. This isn't fatalism; the planning is assumed and expected. It's reassurance that the outcome doesn't rest entirely on getting the plan perfect. Decide carefully, and then hold the plan loosely. The same chapter says 'Commit your deeds to Yahweh, and your plans shall succeed,' so the two ideas belong together: plan seriously, hand the plan over, and don't treat the outcome as a verdict on whether you chose right."},{"id":"psalm-25-4-5","why":"David asks to be shown, taught, and guided, and then says 'I wait for you all day long.' The waiting is part of asking. It's a prayer you can use word for word when you don't yet know what to do."},{"id":"isaiah-30-21","why":"A voice behind you saying 'this is the way, walk in it.' In context it's a promise to a people who'd been ignoring God's guidance and would finally receive it. The image is of correction as you go, not a full itinerary beforehand. That's how most guidance actually works."}],"related":["wisdom","waiting","job-interview","starting-over","patience","moving"]},{"slug":"waiting","url":"/situations/waiting/","title":"Bible Verses for Waiting","h1":"Bible verses for waiting","label":"Waiting","for":"waiting","cluster":"decisions","aliases":["waiting","wait","waiting on god","waiting for an answer","delay","delayed","on hold","limbo","in between","nothing is happening","when will","how long","unanswered prayer","silence"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-27-14","why":"'Wait for Yahweh' appears twice in one verse, with 'be strong and let your heart take courage' between them. The repetition matters: waiting is something you decide again after you've already decided it. The psalm was written under threat, so this isn't waiting in comfort."},{"id":"isaiah-40-31","why":"Strength is renewed in those who wait, not after the waiting ends. The famous images (eagles, running, walking) descend in intensity, and the last one, walking without fainting, is the most relevant to a long wait. The chapter is addressed to exiles who thought God had forgotten them."},{"id":"lamentations-3-25-26","why":"'It is good that a man should hope and quietly wait.' This is in the middle of Lamentations, a book written in ruins. The writer isn't waiting in a pleasant place. He's saying that quiet waiting is good even here, which is a stronger claim than it sounds."},{"id":"psalm-37-7","why":"'Rest in Yahweh, and wait patiently for him' is paired with 'don't fret' about people who seem to be getting ahead. Waiting is hard partly because others don't seem to be. This psalm names that specific irritation and says let it go."},{"id":"habakkuk-2-3","why":"God tells the prophet the answer is coming at an appointed time: 'Though it takes time, wait for it.' The original context is national, but the sentence is about any promise that's true and not yet visible. Delay is admitted openly; the delay isn't the same as denial."},{"id":"micah-7-7","why":"'I will look. I will wait. My God will hear me.' Three short sentences of resolve, from a prophet surrounded by corruption with no reason for optimism. It's a good verse to say out loud when the waiting has gone on longer than you thought it would."},{"id":"psalm-40-1-2","why":"'I waited patiently' and then, in past tense, God heard, lifted, set on rock. It's testimony from the far side of a wait. Reading it doesn't shorten yours, but it tells you what the other side has looked like for someone before."}],"related":["patience","waiting-for-test-results","hope","difficult-decisions","job-loss","peace"]},{"slug":"cannot-sleep","url":"/situations/cannot-sleep/","title":"Bible Verses When You Can't Sleep","h1":"Bible verses when you can't sleep","label":"Can't sleep","for":"when you can't sleep","cluster":"feelings","aliases":["sleep","can't sleep","cant sleep","insomnia","awake","up at night","late at night","middle of the night","3am","3 am","nightmares","night","tonight","bedtime","bed","restless night","lying awake"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-4-8","why":"A bedtime psalm. David says he'll both lie down and sleep, and the reason is that God alone makes him live in safety. Earlier in the psalm, people are lying about him and he's under pressure. He isn't sleeping because everything's resolved. He's sleeping because he's not the one on watch."},{"id":"psalm-127-2","why":"'It is vain to rise up early, to stay up late,' eating 'the bread of toil,' and then: he gives sleep to those he loves. It's one of the only verses where sleep itself is called a gift. If you're awake because you're trying to solve something, this verse says the solving isn't all on you."},{"id":"proverbs-3-23-24","why":"'When you lie down, you will not be afraid. Yes, you will lie down, and your sleep will be sweet.' In Proverbs this is the result of keeping wisdom close. Sweet sleep is treated as a normal consequence of a settled mind, which tells you where the writers thought insomnia comes from."},{"id":"psalm-63-6","why":"'When I remember you on my bed, and think about you in the night watches.' David was awake at night too. The difference is what he did with the wakefulness: he used it to remember. If you can't sleep anyway, this verse suggests what to think about instead of the thing keeping you up."},{"id":"matthew-11-28-29","why":"'I will give you rest' is a promise from Jesus to the heavily burdened. The rest he offers is 'for your souls,' which is often where sleeplessness starts. If you're tired in a way that sleep alone wouldn't fix, this is the passage."},{"id":"philippians-4-6-7","why":"Middle-of-the-night worry has a specific shape: a problem you can't act on for hours. Paul's instruction is to turn each worry into a request. 'Guard your hearts and your thoughts' is what a mind at 2 a.m. needs most. Paul adds 'with thanksgiving,' which is easy to skip past. Naming even one thing you're grateful for changes the shape of a 2 a.m. thought loop. Peace here is described as a guard, something posted at the door of your mind."},{"id":"psalm-121-1-2","why":"The psalm that follows this (verses 3-4) says the one who keeps you 'will neither slumber nor sleep.' Someone is awake so you don't have to be. It's a pilgrim song for travelers sleeping in unfamiliar places, which is close to what an unfamiliar night feels like."}],"related":["worry","anxiety","peace","overwhelmed","loneliness","waiting-for-test-results"]},{"slug":"starting-over","url":"/situations/starting-over/","title":"Bible Verses for Starting Over","h1":"Bible verses for starting over","label":"Starting over","for":"starting over","cluster":"changes","aliases":["starting over","start over","new beginning","new beginnings","fresh start","begin again","rebuild","rebuilding","restart","after divorce","divorce","breakup","broke up","second chance","new chapter","new season","change","changes","transition","new start"],"verses":[{"id":"isaiah-43-18-19","why":"'Don't remember the former things' and 'I will do a new thing' were spoken to exiles who couldn't stop looking back at what they'd lost. God doesn't erase the past; he announces something new and asks, 'Don't you know it?' as if it's already sprouting. A way in the wilderness is exactly what starting over feels like."},{"id":"lamentations-3-22-23","why":"'New every morning' is the line, and it comes from a book of grief. The writer has watched his city burn. Mercies that reset daily are what you need when a whole life doesn't reset at once. Starting over is mostly a series of mornings."},{"id":"philippians-3-13-14","why":"Paul's own starting over. He'd built an entire identity that he then left behind. 'Forgetting the things which are behind' isn't amnesia; it's refusing to let the past be the thing that defines the direction. 'Stretching forward' is the posture of someone leaning into the next thing."},{"id":"2-corinthians-5-17","why":"'The old things have passed away. Behold, all things have become new.' This is about what happens when a person belongs to Christ, and it's the most complete starting-over statement in the Bible. If the thing you need to leave behind is a version of yourself, this is the verse."},{"id":"genesis-12-1","why":"Abraham's story begins with leaving: country, relatives, father's house, destination unknown. He was seventy-five. Every major story in the Bible flows from this one act of starting over late and without a map. That's worth remembering if you feel too old or too unsure."},{"id":"psalm-40-1-2","why":"Out of the pit, out of the mud, feet set on rock. It's past tense, someone describing how a bad season ended. The order matters: he waited, God heard, then the lifting. Starting over often begins before you can see where you're standing."},{"id":"joshua-1-9","why":"Joshua is starting over as leader of a whole nation, replacing Moses, with a river and hostile territory in front of him. 'Be strong and courageous' comes with 'God is with you wherever you go.' New beginnings are frightening; the promise is company, not ease."}],"related":["hope","courage","job-loss","moving","waiting","forgiveness"]},{"slug":"difficult-coworkers","url":"/situations/difficult-coworkers/","title":"Bible Verses for Difficult Coworkers","h1":"Bible verses for difficult coworkers","label":"Difficult coworkers","for":"difficult coworkers","cluster":"work","aliases":["coworker","coworkers","co-worker","co-workers","colleague","colleagues","boss","my boss","manager","difficult people","difficult person","rude","office","toxic workplace","toxic","people at work","someone at work","workplace","team"],"verses":[{"id":"proverbs-15-1","why":"'A gentle answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger.' This is a proverb about escalation and how to stop it. It doesn't say the other person will become reasonable. It says your reply is a lever, and you get to choose which way to pull it."},{"id":"romans-12-17-18","why":"'If it is possible, as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men.' Paul builds in two qualifiers because he knows peace isn't always possible and isn't entirely yours to make. What is yours: not repaying evil for evil, and being honorable in a way everyone can see. That's a workplace ethic in one sentence."},{"id":"proverbs-19-11","why":"'It is his glory to overlook an offense.' Overlooking isn't the same as being a doormat; it's choosing which battles are worth having. With a coworker who gives you daily small reasons to be offended, this proverb is practical: most of them can be let go, and letting them go is a strength."},{"id":"james-1-19","why":"Swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to anger. In that order. With difficult people, the temptation is to reverse it. James writes this to a community with real conflicts, and the advice hasn't aged. The slowness is the skill."},{"id":"colossians-3-13","why":"'Bearing with one another' is the phrase for tolerating ongoing friction. 'If any man has a complaint against any' assumes you will have one. The standard for forgiving is how Christ forgave you, which is a high bar, and Paul knew it."},{"id":"colossians-3-23-24","why":"'Whatever you do, work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men.' When a coworker makes the job feel pointless or unfair, this verse changes who you're working for. It's addressed to servants in the first century, people with far less control over their work than you have."},{"id":"proverbs-16-32","why":"Ruling your own spirit is ranked above conquering a city. In a tense office, keeping your temper feels like the smaller achievement. The proverb says it's the bigger one. The verse pairs 'slow to anger' with 'rules his spirit,' the same skill seen from outside and inside. Nobody sees the emails you didn't send or the comeback you didn't make. Proverbs says those unseen victories are the bigger ones."}],"related":["work-stress","patience","forgiveness","difficult-family-members","wisdom","peace"]},{"slug":"work-stress","url":"/situations/work-stress/","title":"Bible Verses for Work Stress","h1":"Bible verses for work stress","label":"Work stress","for":"work stress","cluster":"work","aliases":["work","job","work stress","stressful job","deadline","deadlines","meeting","big meeting","presentation","workload","overworked","working too much","hate my job","monday","long week","busy"],"verses":[{"id":"matthew-11-28-29","why":"'All you who labor and are heavily burdened' is a direct address to the overworked. Jesus doesn't offer to remove the work. He offers rest 'for your souls' and a yoke that's easy, which in farming means one that fits. Stress often means carrying a yoke that doesn't."},{"id":"colossians-3-23-24","why":"'Work heartily, as for the Lord, and not for men.' This reframes who the work is for, which changes how much the boss's mood or the review cycle can dictate your peace. First written to servants, it's the Bible's clearest statement that ordinary work has dignity."},{"id":"psalm-127-2","why":"Rising early, staying late, eating 'the bread of toil' is called vain here, not because effort is bad but because anxious effort forgets who provides. 'He gives sleep to his loved ones' is the correction. If work has eaten your sleep, this psalm is about you."},{"id":"psalm-90-17","why":"'Establish the work of our hands' is a prayer that what you do would last and matter. Moses prays it after reflecting on how short life is. It's a good sentence to say on the way into a hard day: let this count."},{"id":"galatians-6-9","why":"'Let's not be weary in doing good.' Paul admits weariness is the risk and answers it with a harvest 'in due season,' which means the payoff has a timeline you don't control. For work that feels unrewarded right now, the promise is that it's not unseen."},{"id":"philippians-4-6-7","why":"Work stress tends to be a list of specific worries. Paul's instruction fits a list: take each one and make it a request, with thanks. The peace that follows 'guards' your thoughts, which is what a mind spinning on Sunday night needs."},{"id":"proverbs-16-3","why":"'Commit your deeds to Yahweh, and your plans shall succeed.' The Hebrew for 'commit' means roll onto, as if you're rolling a weight off yourself. It's not a guarantee of every project working out. It's a picture of handing over the outcome so you can do the work without carrying the whole result."}],"related":["difficult-coworkers","overwhelmed","job-interview","job-loss","cannot-sleep","peace"]},{"slug":"job-interview","url":"/situations/job-interview/","title":"Bible Verses Before a Job Interview","h1":"Bible verses before a job interview","label":"Job interview","for":"a job interview","cluster":"work","aliases":["interview","job interview","interviewing","job search","applying","application","hiring","new job","first day","starting a new job","promotion","public speaking","speech","presenting"],"verses":[{"id":"exodus-4-12","why":"Moses told God he wasn't a good speaker. God's answer: 'Go, and I will be with your mouth, and teach you what you shall speak.' The verse doesn't promise eloquence; it promises company in the speaking. For the moment before an interview, that's the right promise."},{"id":"philippians-4-6-7","why":"Interview nerves are anxiety with a start time. Paul's instruction fits: name the specific worry, ask, give thanks, and let peace stand guard over your thoughts. This is the verse to read in the parking lot. 'With thanksgiving' is the part people skip. Being grateful you got the interview at all shrinks the fear of the interview. The peace Paul describes doesn't remove nerves; it keeps them from running the room."},{"id":"proverbs-16-3","why":"'Commit your deeds to Yahweh, and your plans shall succeed.' The word for 'commit' means roll onto, as in rolling a weight off yourself. It's not a guarantee this specific job is yours. It's a way to walk in without carrying the whole outcome."},{"id":"joshua-1-9","why":"'Be strong and courageous' was said to a man about to take on something far bigger than a job. The reason attached, 'God is with you wherever you go,' includes conference rooms. Courage in this verse is derived from presence, not personality."},{"id":"colossians-3-23-24","why":"Work 'as for the Lord.' It helps to remember who you're ultimately working for before you sit across from someone deciding whether to hire you. It lowers the stakes of the room without lowering your effort. Paul wrote this to people at the very bottom of the labor market, who had no control over their employers. If they could work with that kind of freedom, an interview candidate can walk in with some of it too."},{"id":"psalm-90-17","why":"'Establish the work of our hands.' A short prayer for the morning of: let what I've prepared hold up. Moses prayed it after admitting how brief life is, which is a decent antidote to treating one interview as everything. The psalm is attributed to Moses and is mostly about how short life is. Ending it with a request that our work would last is a quiet kind of confidence: not that this interview will go perfectly, but that honest work isn't wasted."},{"id":"james-1-5","why":"You'll be asked questions you didn't prepare for. James says wisdom is available for the asking, given generously and without reproach. It's a good verse to have in mind when you need to think on your feet. The wisdom James means is practical, how to act well under pressure, not trivia. The verse also promises it's given 'without reproach.' You won't be made to feel foolish for asking, which is more than most interviews promise."}],"related":["starting-over","courage","work-stress","job-loss","wisdom","waiting"]},{"slug":"job-loss","url":"/situations/job-loss/","title":"Bible Verses After Losing a Job","h1":"Bible verses after losing a job","label":"Job loss","for":"after losing a job","cluster":"work","aliases":["lost my job","laid off","layoff","layoffs","fired","let go from work","unemployed","unemployment","out of work","job loss","losing my job","redundant","redundancy","no job"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-34-18","why":"'Yahweh is near to those who have a broken heart.' Job loss can break something, especially when it wasn't your choice. This verse doesn't offer a plan; it offers nearness, and it's addressed specifically to the crushed. That's the first thing to hear, before any verse about provision."},{"id":"matthew-6-26","why":"Birds don't have jobs and are fed anyway. Jesus asks whether you're worth more than they are, and the answer is yes. The verse doesn't tell you not to look for work. It tells you your value and your provision were never contingent on the position you just lost."},{"id":"philippians-4-19","why":"'My God will supply every need of yours.' Paul wrote this from prison, to people who'd sent him support. The supply is 'according to his riches,' not according to your severance. It's about need, and it's honest about the source."},{"id":"isaiah-43-18-19","why":"'Don't remember the former things... I will do a new thing.' Spoken to people who'd lost everything familiar. Job loss forces a new thing whether you wanted one or not; this verse says God is already at work in it, 'springing out now,' even before you can see it."},{"id":"romans-8-28","why":"'All things work together for good for those who love God.' Be careful with this one: it doesn't say the layoff was good, and it doesn't promise the next job will be better. It says God works through all of it, including the bad parts, toward good. That's the accurate reading, and it's enough."},{"id":"psalm-37-25","why":"An old man's testimony: he hasn't seen the righteous forsaken. It's one person's long-life observation, not a legal guarantee, but it's the kind of thing you need to hear from someone who's watched a lot of people go through a lot of things."},{"id":"deuteronomy-31-8","why":"'Yahweh himself is who goes before you.' The verse ends with 'don't be afraid, don't be discouraged,' which are the two things job loss produces most reliably. The promise is that the next stretch has already been walked ahead of you."}],"related":["financial-stress","starting-over","worry","hope","job-interview","waiting"]},{"slug":"retirement","url":"/situations/retirement/","title":"Bible Verses for Retirement","h1":"Bible verses for retirement","label":"Retirement","for":"retirement","cluster":"changes","aliases":["retirement","retire","retiring","retired","getting old","old age","aging","later years","empty nest","grandparent","grandchildren","purpose","what now"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-92-14","why":"'They will still produce fruit in old age.' The psalm compares the righteous to trees planted in God's house that stay green. It's a direct answer to the fear that retirement means becoming useless. Fruit in old age is the expectation, not the exception."},{"id":"isaiah-46-4","why":"'Even to old age I am he, and even to gray hairs I will carry you.' God contrasts himself with idols that have to be carried around. He carries. For a season where you're no longer the one holding everything up at work, this is the right verse."},{"id":"2-timothy-4-7","why":"'I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith.' Paul, near the end. Retirement is a course finished, and this is what finishing well sounds like. The verse is often read at funerals but fits any ending done with integrity."},{"id":"psalm-71-18","why":"An older person's prayer: don't forsake me until I've told the next generation what you've done. This verse gives retirement a job: pass it on. Whatever you learned over a career is meant to be declared to someone younger. The psalmist is honest about being 'old and gray-haired' and asks not to be abandoned. Then the request turns outward: let me declare your strength to the next generation. Retirement is often when people finally have time to do exactly that."},{"id":"ecclesiastes-3-1","why":"'For everything there is a season.' The famous list that follows includes planting and uprooting, keeping and throwing away. Retirement is a season change, and Ecclesiastes gives you permission to treat it as one rather than an ending. The list that follows pairs each thing with its opposite: build and break down, keep silent and speak. None of them is called wrong. A season of working hard and a season of not working are both, in this view, appropriate in their time."},{"id":"philippians-1-6","why":"'He who began a good work in you will complete it.' The work God is doing in a person doesn't stop when employment does. Paul is confident about completion, and the timeline extends to 'the day of Christ Jesus,' well past any retirement date."},{"id":"psalm-90-12","why":"'Teach us to count our days, that we may gain a heart of wisdom.' Retirement makes the counting real. The verse turns that into an asset: knowing time is limited produces wisdom about what to do with it. Moses wrote this psalm reflecting on how short a life is. Counting days sounds grim, but the verse treats it as the path to wisdom. People who know their time is limited tend to spend it on what matters, which is a good way to retire."}],"related":["retirement-card","starting-over","wisdom","difficult-family-members","hope","waiting"]},{"slug":"before-surgery","url":"/situations/before-surgery/","title":"Bible Verses Before Surgery","h1":"Bible verses before surgery","label":"Before surgery","for":"before surgery","cluster":"health","aliases":["surgery","operation","before surgery","having surgery","hospital","in the hospital","procedure","recovery","recovering","healing","heal","sick","illness","ill","cancer","diagnosis","diagnosed","chemo","pain","chronic pain","health","unwell","not well"],"verses":[{"id":"isaiah-41-10","why":"'Don't be afraid, for I am with you.' Five promises in one verse, ending with being held up by God's right hand. It's addressed to people with real reasons to fear. It's the verse most often written on a card and slipped into a hospital bag."},{"id":"psalm-23-4","why":"'Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me.' The psalm doesn't say the valley is avoided. It says the walk through it isn't alone. Before surgery, that's the honest version of comfort."},{"id":"philippians-4-6-7","why":"Pre-surgery anxiety comes with specific questions. Paul's instruction is to make each one a request, then let peace stand guard. The peace 'surpasses understanding,' meaning it can be present even when the outcome is unknown. Paul's instruction has a structure that helps in a pre-op bed: take each specific fear, turn it into a specific request, and add one thing you're thankful for. The peace he describes stands guard over your thoughts; it doesn't require the outcome to be known."},{"id":"psalm-46-1","why":"'A very present help in trouble.' Present means now, not later. This is the verse for the pre-op room, when help needs to be immediate and you can't do anything but lie still. The psalm goes on to picture the earth changing and mountains shaking, then says 'Be still, and know that I am God.' Surgery involves handing control to other people. This psalm was written for people who had no control either."},{"id":"exodus-33-14","why":"'My presence will go with you, and I will give you rest.' Moses asked for exactly this and got it. Going into an operating room, you go under, and rest is literally what happens. This verse promises the presence goes in with you."},{"id":"psalm-4-8","why":"'In peace I will both lay myself down and sleep, for you, Yahweh alone, make me live in safety.' Written for a normal bedtime, it fits the night before surgery unusually well: lying down, sleeping, being kept safe by someone who's awake."},{"id":"psalm-73-26","why":"'My flesh and my heart fails, but God is the strength of my heart and my portion forever.' The verse admits the body fails. That admission is what makes the second half believable. It's for someone whose body is the reason they're in this room."}],"related":["hope","waiting-for-test-results","worry","anxiety","peace","courage"]},{"slug":"waiting-for-test-results","url":"/situations/waiting-for-test-results/","title":"Bible Verses While Waiting for Test Results","h1":"Bible verses while waiting for test results","label":"Waiting for test results","for":"waiting for test results","cluster":"health","aliases":["test results","results","waiting for results","biopsy","scan","mri","blood test","lab results","doctor","doctors","waiting on the doctor","bad news","medical","waiting for news"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-112-7","why":"'He will not be afraid of evil news. His heart is steadfast, trusting in Yahweh.' A verse about bad news, written before the news arrives. The steadiness isn't from knowing the result; it's from trusting regardless. Few verses fit this exact situation as well."},{"id":"psalm-56-3","why":"'When I am afraid, I will put my trust in you.' Not if; when. David wrote it as a captive. The verse assumes fear will come in waves and gives one thing to do each time it does. The next verse adds 'I will not be afraid,' which sounds like a contradiction until you see it as a sequence. Fear arrives, trust is chosen, fear loses its grip. Then it arrives again. David's psalm is honest that this is a cycle, not a cure."},{"id":"philippians-4-6-7","why":"Every possible result generates its own worry. Paul's instruction is to bring each one as a request, with thanks. The peace that follows guards your thoughts, which during a wait like this run in circles. 'In everything' is the key phrase for a wait like this, because the mind produces a new scenario every hour. Each one can be handed over in turn. Peace that 'surpasses understanding' means it can come before the results do."},{"id":"psalm-46-10","why":"'Be still, and know that I am God.' In context it's said in the middle of upheaval, with nations raging. Being still while waiting for a result isn't passivity; it's a decision to stop rehearsing outcomes. The knowing is the anchor."},{"id":"isaiah-26-3","why":"'Perfect peace' for the one whose mind is fixed. A mind waiting for results is the opposite of fixed; it jumps between scenarios. This verse names the alternative: keep bringing the mind back to the same place. Isaiah wrote this as a song for a city under threat. The word for 'stayed' means propped up or leaned. A mind can't stop imagining outcomes, but it can keep being leaned back onto something steadier than the next phone call."},{"id":"psalm-27-14","why":"'Wait for Yahweh. Be strong, and let your heart take courage. Yes, wait.' Waiting said twice, with courage in the middle. It's the verse for the day the results were supposed to come and didn't. The whole psalm is David steadying himself, moving between confidence and fear. It ends here, with waiting as the last word. He doesn't say the waiting will be short or that the news will be good. He says it can be done with courage."},{"id":"romans-8-26","why":"'We don't know how to pray as we ought, but the Spirit himself makes intercession for us with groanings which can't be uttered.' Waiting for a diagnosis, you may not know what to ask for. This verse says you don't have to. The groaning is the prayer."}],"related":["before-surgery","waiting","anxiety","peace","cannot-sleep","worry"]},{"slug":"moving","url":"/situations/moving/","title":"Bible Verses for Moving","h1":"Bible verses for moving","label":"Moving","for":"moving","cluster":"changes","aliases":["moving","move","new home","new house","new city","relocating","relocation","new apartment","leaving home","far from home","homesick","home"],"verses":[{"id":"genesis-28-15","why":"Jacob is sleeping on the ground, running from his brother, on his way to a country he's never seen. God says: 'I am with you, and will keep you, wherever you go.' The promise is for the road and for the arrival. It's the verse for a moving van."},{"id":"genesis-12-1","why":"'Leave your country, and your relatives, and your father's house.' Abraham's move is the first act of the whole biblical story. He went without knowing the destination. If your move involves leaving people you love, this is the passage that takes that seriously."},{"id":"psalm-90-1","why":"'Lord, you have been our dwelling place for all generations.' Moses wrote this without a permanent home, wandering for forty years. Home, in this psalm, is a person rather than a place. That's the verse for the week when you don't have an address yet."},{"id":"deuteronomy-31-8","why":"'Yahweh himself is who goes before you.' The new city has already been walked ahead of you. The verse ends with 'don't be afraid, don't be discouraged,' which are the two moods of unpacking boxes in a place you don't know."},{"id":"psalm-139-9-10","why":"'If I settle in the uttermost parts of the sea, even there your hand will lead me.' The psalmist imagines the most remote move possible and finds God there too. Distance from what's familiar isn't distance from God. The psalm has been listing places you could go to get away from God, and coming up empty each time. For a move, that's a comfort rather than a threat: the person who is leading you there is already there."},{"id":"psalm-127-1","why":"'Unless Yahweh builds the house, they who build it labor in vain.' A verse for the arrival: the new home is a house until something more is built in it. It's a good line to read aloud on the first night."},{"id":"hebrews-11-8","why":"Abraham 'went out, not knowing where he went.' The writer of Hebrews calls this faith. If your move feels like a leap, Scripture has a category for it, and it's a positive one. Abraham left an established city for a promise with no map. Hebrews lists that as the first thing he did right. You don't need to know how a move will turn out for it to be an act of trust rather than recklessness."}],"related":["hope","starting-over","safe-travel","loneliness","courage","waiting"]},{"slug":"graduation","url":"/situations/graduation/","title":"Bible Verses for Graduation","h1":"Bible verses for graduation","label":"Graduation","for":"graduation","cluster":"changes","aliases":["graduation","graduate","graduating","graduated","college","university","school","student","exams","exam","finals","test","studying","senior","diploma","degree"],"verses":[{"id":"proverbs-3-5-6","why":"'Trust in Yahweh with all your heart, and don't lean on your own understanding.' For a graduate who's just spent years building understanding, this is a well-timed word: use it, but don't make it the foundation. 'He will make your paths straight' is for the years when the path isn't obvious."},{"id":"jeremiah-29-11","why":"The most common graduation verse, and worth knowing its setting: it was written to exiles who'd be in Babylon for seventy years. The 'hope and a future' were real but slow. That makes it a better graduation verse, not a worse one. The future comes, but it's a long game."},{"id":"1-timothy-4-12","why":"'Let no one despise your youth; but be an example.' Paul to a young leader who'd be doubted because of his age. It's the verse for a graduate walking into rooms where they're the youngest. The instruction isn't to demand respect but to earn it in five specific ways."},{"id":"joshua-1-9","why":"Joshua, just promoted, told to be strong and courageous because God goes with him 'wherever you go.' A graduate is at the same kind of threshold: new responsibility, unfamiliar territory, and the same promise. The 'wherever' matters because Joshua was about to lead people into territory none of them had seen. The verse repeats 'be strong and courageous' three times in a few lines, which suggests God knew how a person feels at a threshold."},{"id":"philippians-1-6","why":"'He who began a good work in you will complete it.' Graduation is a completion that's also a beginning. This verse says the deeper work in a person keeps going, and God finishes what he starts. Paul wrote this to a church he founded and loved, and he's confident about them because of God, not because of their track record. For a graduate, it's reassurance that the person you're becoming isn't finished, and isn't left to chance."},{"id":"micah-6-8","why":"'Act justly, love mercy, walk humbly.' If a graduate wanted one sentence to carry into a career, this is the one many people would choose. It's what God requires, stated plainly, with no degree needed. Micah delivers it after asking what God could possibly want, whether thousands of rams or rivers of oil, and the answer is disarmingly simple. Justice, mercy, humility. A career built on those three will outlast most of what a degree teaches."},{"id":"psalm-32-8","why":"'I will instruct you and teach you in the way which you shall go.' For someone who's just finished being taught, a promise that instruction continues, from a teacher watching closely. The psalm is David's, written after a season of hiding a wrong and then owning it. The teaching he describes comes with 'my eye upon you,' which is a picture of attention, not surveillance. Someone is still watching how you go."}],"related":["graduation-card","wisdom","starting-over","difficult-decisions","courage","job-interview"]},{"slug":"safe-travel","url":"/situations/safe-travel/","title":"Bible Verses for Safe Travel","h1":"Bible verses for safe travel","label":"Safe travel","for":"safe travel","cluster":"changes","aliases":["travel","traveling","travelling","trip","flight","flying","fly","road trip","journey","drive","driving","vacation","deployment","deployed","safe travels","safe trip","airport","going away"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-121-7-8","why":"'Yahweh will keep your going out and your coming in.' This is the traveler's psalm, and these are its last lines. 'Going out and coming in' covers the whole trip: departure and return. It's the most-quoted travel blessing for good reason."},{"id":"psalm-121-1-2","why":"The opening of the same psalm: lifting eyes to the hills, which for a pilgrim were both the route and the danger. Help comes from the one 'who made heaven and earth,' which is to say the one who made the terrain."},{"id":"genesis-28-15","why":"God's promise to Jacob, alone on a road: 'I am with you, and will keep you, wherever you go, and will bring you again.' The 'bring you again' is the return trip. It's a complete travel promise: there, kept, and back."},{"id":"psalm-91-11","why":"'He will put his angels in charge of you, to guard you in all your ways.' 'All your ways' includes the highway. This is the psalm people read before flights. It's a picture of being watched over en route. The psalm was written for people facing real dangers: plague, arrows, lions. It doesn't say nothing will happen. It says God assigns guardians. Read the whole psalm and you'll find it is honest about hazards while being confident about protection."},{"id":"psalm-139-9-10","why":"The wings of the dawn, the far side of the sea: the psalmist imagines the farthest journey possible and finds God's hand there. For long-distance travel, this is the verse that says distance changes nothing. The psalm has been trying, verse by verse, to find somewhere God isn't, and failing. Applied to travel, it means the destination, however far, isn't outside the range of the hand that leads you."},{"id":"isaiah-43-2","why":"Waters, rivers, fire. The verse doesn't promise you'll avoid them; it promises you'll pass through. Spoken to exiles, it's honest that journeys involve hazards, and it puts God in the hazard with you. The verse begins 'when you pass through,' not 'if.' Isaiah writes it to people who have already been through the worst. The promise is presence rather than avoidance, which is the honest kind of promise for a journey."},{"id":"numbers-6-24-26","why":"The oldest blessing in Scripture, and 'keep you' is its second phrase. It's the thing to say to someone as they leave. Short enough to text, old enough that it's been said over travelers for three thousand years. Aaron and his sons were told to say these exact words over the people. 'Keep' in this blessing means guard and preserve. If you want something to say at the door before someone drives away, this is what generations have said."}],"related":["moving","courage","peace","anxiety","waiting","graduation"]},{"slug":"marriage","url":"/situations/marriage/","title":"Bible Verses for Marriage","h1":"Bible verses for marriage","label":"Marriage","for":"marriage","cluster":"family","aliases":["marriage","married","husband","wife","spouse","wedding","anniversary","engaged","engagement","relationship","partner","love","couple","my marriage"],"verses":[{"id":"1-corinthians-13-4-7","why":"Read at nearly every wedding, and best understood as a checklist for the years after. Paul wrote it to a church in conflict, not to a couple, which is why it's so practical: patient, kind, not keeping score, bearing all things. Replace 'love' with your own name and see how it reads."},{"id":"ecclesiastes-4-9-10","why":"'Two are better than one' because when one falls, the other lifts them up. It's an unsentimental description of what a marriage is for. Verse 12, the threefold cord, extends it: two people with God as the third strand don't break easily."},{"id":"colossians-3-14","why":"'Above all these things, walk in love, which is the bond of perfection.' The preceding verses list bearing with each other and forgiving. Love is what holds the list together. For a marriage in a hard patch, this verse says what to put on top."},{"id":"ephesians-4-2-3","why":"'Bearing with one another in love, being eager to keep the unity.' 'Eager' is the word. Staying united isn't automatic; it takes someone wanting it. Paul wrote this about a church, and it applies to any two people who've promised to stay."},{"id":"ephesians-4-26","why":"'Don't let the sun go down on your wrath.' Anger is allowed ('be angry, and don't sin'), but it has a deadline. Couples have used this as a rule for centuries because it works: deal with it today. The line sits in a passage about putting away lies and speaking truth to each other, because 'we are members of one another.' In a marriage, unresolved anger doesn't stay in one person. Paul's deadline keeps it from moving in permanently."},{"id":"1-peter-4-8","why":"'Love covers a multitude of sins.' Not one or two; a multitude. This isn't a license for bad behavior. It's a description of what love does with the ordinary daily failures that pile up in any home. Peter writes to people under real pressure and tells them, above all, to keep loving each other 'earnestly,' a word for a stretched-out effort. Covering sins doesn't mean pretending they didn't happen; it means not keeping score."},{"id":"proverbs-17-17","why":"'A friend loves at all times.' A spouse who is also a friend loves in the seasons that aren't romantic. This proverb is about steadiness, which is most of what a long marriage is made of. The second half says 'a brother is born for adversity,' which is the same idea from another angle. Someone is meant to be there when things go badly. A marriage where each is that person for the other has what this verse describes."}],"related":["forgiveness","patience","difficult-family-members","peace","loneliness","hope"]},{"slug":"difficult-family-members","url":"/situations/difficult-family-members/","title":"Bible Verses for Difficult Family Members","h1":"Bible verses for difficult family members","label":"Difficult family","for":"difficult family members","cluster":"family","aliases":["family","mother","father","mom","dad","parents","sister","brother","in-laws","in laws","mother in law","mother-in-law","relatives","family drama","family conflict","estranged","adult children","my son","my daughter","kids","children","teenager","holidays with family","difficult family"],"verses":[{"id":"romans-12-17-18","why":"'If it is possible, as much as it is up to you, be at peace with all men.' Paul admits peace isn't always possible and isn't entirely up to you. With family, that honesty matters. Your responsibility is your part: don't repay evil, act honorably. The other person's part isn't yours to carry."},{"id":"proverbs-17-14","why":"'Stop contention before quarreling breaks out.' The image is a dam breaching: once the water's moving, you can't stop it. Family arguments have a familiar early moment where things could still go either way. This proverb says leave then. The verse is about timing. It doesn't ask you to win the argument or avoid the person forever. It asks you to notice the moment before the moment, when a subject change or a walk to the kitchen still works."},{"id":"proverbs-15-1","why":"'A gentle answer turns away wrath.' With relatives, you often know exactly what will set them off. This proverb puts the lever in your hand: your reply can escalate or de-escalate, and you get to choose, even when they don't."},{"id":"colossians-3-13","why":"'Bearing with one another, and forgiving each other, if any man has a complaint against any.' Bearing with is tolerating the ongoing friction; forgiving is releasing the specific wrong. Family requires both, repeatedly. The standard is Christ's forgiveness of you, which is meant to be humbling, not crushing."},{"id":"1-peter-3-9","why":"'Not rendering evil for evil, or insult for insult; but instead blessing.' Peter is realistic about insults. Family insults land harder because they know where to aim. The instruction isn't to absorb them silently but to respond with blessing, which is a choice, not a feeling."},{"id":"proverbs-19-11","why":"'It is his glory to overlook an offense.' Not every remark at a family dinner needs a response. Overlooking is a skill and, this proverb says, an honor. It's also the only way to survive some holidays. Discretion in the first half of the verse is what makes overlooking possible: knowing which remarks are worth answering. Most aren't. Proverbs treats restraint as a mark of maturity, not weakness."},{"id":"ephesians-4-26","why":"'Be angry, and don't sin.' The anger is allowed; it's what you do with it. The deadline, before the sun goes down, keeps a single argument from becoming a decades-long estrangement. Many family rifts began as one unresolved evening. Paul's next line is 'don't give place to the devil,' which is his way of saying that anger left overnight becomes a foothold. The verse doesn't require the argument to be resolved by bedtime, only that it not be abandoned there."}],"related":["forgiveness","patience","difficult-coworkers","marriage","peace","loneliness"]},{"slug":"retirement-card","url":"/occasions/retirement-card/","title":"Bible Verse for a Retirement Card","h1":"Bible verse for a retirement card","label":"Retirement card","for":"a retirement card","cluster":"occasions","aliases":["retirement card","card for retirement","retirement gift","retirement party","someone retiring","coworker retiring","retiring coworker"],"verses":[{"id":"2-timothy-4-7","why":"'I have fought the good fight. I have finished the course. I have kept the faith.' Paul, near the end of his life, looking back. It's the standard retirement verse because it honors a course completed with integrity. It works for a colleague you respect and for a parent."},{"id":"matthew-25-21","why":"'Well done, good and faithful servant... Enter into the joy of your lord.' From a parable about servants entrusted with money. The line is often quoted at retirements because it praises faithfulness over results and ends with joy. It's the traditional choice, and it lands."},{"id":"psalm-92-14","why":"'They will still produce fruit in old age.' The right verse for someone who's worried retirement means fading out. It says the opposite: green, full of sap, still bearing. Encouraging without being saccharine. The psalm compares the righteous to a palm tree and a cedar, both slow-growing and long-lived. For a card, the image of continued fruitfulness says what most people want to hear at a retirement: this isn't the end of your usefulness."},{"id":"numbers-6-24-26","why":"The priestly blessing, three lines, ending in peace. It fits on any card and has been said over people at thresholds for three thousand years. If you're not sure, use this. It works because it doesn't try to summarize a career. It simply asks for blessing, protection, favor, and peace over the next stretch. Traditional enough for a formal card, warm enough for a close colleague."},{"id":"ecclesiastes-3-1","why":"'For everything there is a season.' Short and universally known. It frames retirement as a season, which is what most retirees want to hear: not an ending, a change. The verses that follow list pairs: a time to plant and to uproot, to keep and to throw away. Quoting even the first line brings that whole list to mind. It's especially good for someone who seems unsure how to feel about the change."},{"id":"psalm-90-17","why":"'Establish the work of our hands.' A prayer that what someone built would last. For a colleague whose work mattered to you, this says so without gushing. Moses wrote this at the end of a long psalm about how short life is. The request that our work would be established is a good thing to wish for someone whose work is finishing. It honors what they built."},{"id":"philippians-1-6","why":"'He who began a good work in you will complete it.' A reminder that the important work isn't over. Good for someone who's anxious about purpose after the job. Paul wrote it to people he'd worked alongside, which makes it right for a colleague. The completion he has in mind is the person, not the project. A good verse for someone who has tied their identity to the job."}],"related":["retirement","graduation-card","wisdom","hope","starting-over","peace"]},{"slug":"graduation-card","url":"/occasions/graduation-card/","title":"Bible Verse for a Graduation Card","h1":"Bible verse for a graduation card","label":"Graduation card","for":"a graduation card","cluster":"occasions","aliases":["graduation card","card for graduate","card for graduation","graduation gift","grad card","for a graduate"],"verses":[{"id":"proverbs-3-5-6","why":"'Trust in Yahweh with all your heart... he will make your paths straight.' The right verse for a graduate about to make a lot of decisions. It's well-known, fits on a card, and says something true about the years ahead."},{"id":"jeremiah-29-11","why":"The most-used graduation verse. Worth knowing: it was written to exiles with seventy years of waiting ahead. That doesn't make it a bad choice; it makes it a patient one. 'Hope and a future' is a promise with a long horizon."},{"id":"1-timothy-4-12","why":"'Let no one despise your youth; but be an example.' For a graduate heading into rooms where they'll be the youngest person. It's encouragement with a challenge built in, which most graduates appreciate more than praise. Paul wrote it to a young leader who would be doubted because of his age. The list that follows (speech, conduct, love, faith, purity) is a practical answer to that doubt. It respects the graduate enough to expect something of them."},{"id":"psalm-32-8","why":"'I will instruct you and teach you in the way which you shall go.' One sentence, God speaking. For someone who's just finished being taught, a reminder that the teaching continues. The 'eye upon you' at the end of the verse is a picture of attention rather than surveillance. For a card, it says the graduate isn't being sent out alone, and it says it in a single clean line."},{"id":"joshua-1-9","why":"'Be strong and courageous... God is with you wherever you go.' 'Wherever you go' is the graduation phrase. It works for a graduate moving away, starting a job, or entering the military. Joshua was stepping into leadership after Moses, which is about as intimidating as a first job gets. The verse is long enough that many people quote only the final clause; that works fine on a card."},{"id":"micah-6-8","why":"'Act justly, love mercy, walk humbly with your God.' If you want to give a graduate one sentence to live by, this is the one many people would choose. It fits on a card and on a life. Micah delivers it as the answer to what God actually wants from a person, after ruling out grand gestures. It's a traditional graduation choice for a reason: it describes a good life in nine words without preaching."},{"id":"philippians-1-6","why":"'He who began a good work in you will complete it.' A completion verse for a completion day, with the reminder that the deeper work keeps going. Paul writes it to people he founded a church with, and his confidence is in God's follow-through. For a graduate, it says the person they're becoming isn't finished, and isn't being left to luck."}],"related":["graduation","retirement-card","wisdom","courage","starting-over","difficult-decisions"]},{"slug":"sympathy-card","url":"/occasions/sympathy-card/","title":"Bible Verse for a Sympathy Card","h1":"Bible verse for a sympathy card","label":"Sympathy card","for":"a sympathy card","cluster":"occasions","aliases":["sympathy","sympathy card","condolence card","condolences","card for a funeral","what to write in a sympathy card","funeral card","memorial card","card for loss","verse for a card","words for grieving friend"],"verses":[{"id":"psalm-34-18","why":"'Yahweh is near to those who have a broken heart, and saves those who have a crushed spirit.' It doesn't explain the loss or promise it makes sense. It says God is near to exactly the state the person is in. That's why it's the best all-around sympathy verse."},{"id":"matthew-5-4","why":"'Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted.' Seven words from Jesus. It doesn't rush the mourning; it blesses it, and promises comfort without a date. Fits any card. It's the second of the Beatitudes, spoken to a crowd, and it's unusual because it calls mourners blessed rather than unfortunate. For a card, that reframing is gentle and true without asking the grieving person to feel anything they don't."},{"id":"psalm-23-4","why":"'Even though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death, I will fear no evil, for you are with me.' The traditional choice, read at most funerals. It names death plainly and answers it with presence, not explanation."},{"id":"2-corinthians-1-3-4","why":"God 'comforts us in all our affliction.' Paul calls him 'the God of all comfort,' and the verse is honest that affliction is real. Good for a card because it's about comfort, not resolution. Paul wrote it after a season so hard he says he despaired of life. That's what makes it a credible sympathy verse. It comes from someone who was comforted in real affliction, not from a distance."},{"id":"revelation-21-4","why":"'He will wipe away every tear from their eyes. Death will be no more.' A verse about the end of grief, set in the future. Use it with someone who shares that hope; it's a promise about what's coming, not a claim about right now."},{"id":"psalm-147-3","why":"'He heals the broken in heart, and binds up their wounds.' One sentence, and the image is a physician bandaging. For a card, it says the wound is real and it's being tended. The psalm is about God gathering the scattered and naming the stars, and this line sits in the middle. It's brief enough to write by hand, and it makes no promise about timing, which is honest in the early days of grief."},{"id":"john-14-1-3","why":"'Don't let your heart be troubled... In my Father's house are many homes.' Jesus to his friends the night before he died. It's the traditional funeral reading for a reason: it's about a place prepared. Best for a family who'd want to hear it in Jesus' own words."}],"related":["grief","loneliness","hope","peace","waiting","overwhelmed"]}],"suggestions":["worry","peace","courage","hope","difficult-decisions","overwhelmed"]};